{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars","id":1542327,"title":"Opinions on 3e Lunars","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Lunar Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-09-17T11:40:59-07:00","id":1542327,"title":"Opinions on 3e Lunars","content":"So, Lunars have been around for a long while now. We've had time to play them or use them in our games. What do you think about them? What do you like or dislike about them? How do they fit into your vision of Creation?<br /> <br /> Personally I'm mostly happy about them. They're fun to play or to insert in a story, they're useful in a mixed group without stealing the other Exalted's spotlight.<br /> Their new focus on playing intermediaries between spirits, fae and mortals is also really interesting to explore and it gives Lunars are peace time function that use the liminality of their nature.<br /> Lunar witches have become especially fun with their expended ability to transform people other than themselves and create all kind of crazy situations.<br /> The different Lunar factions and their politicking are a very nice addition, as is the War for the Caul which make for a nice little sandbox, although the Caul itself could do with a little more fleshing out, but we might see that in a book on the West with any luck. I love the concept of Shayan-yas and how it helps make Elder Lunars relevant and helps with creating rivalries or alliances.<br /> <br /> I'm a bit disappointed about how shapeshifted Lunars can't use Martial Arts, even those MA created by Lunars, it feels kinda random and arbitrary, but it's nothing a house rule can't easily fix.<br /> I also wonder why they lost the ability to take the shape of supernatural creatures. It's a bit weird that the shapeshifters made to fight demons, dark gods and behemoths can't turn into one of those beings. That's plenty of lost story potential.<br /> Lastly I believe Lunars still feel more like powerful Terrestrial Exalted than like Celestials. They're strong and versatile, yes, but they don't have the cosmic scope Solars, Abyssals, Infernals, Sidereals or even Getimians have. They don't shape fate, reality or the Chaos at the edge of the world, they don't shake the fundations of Heaven or shatter constellations, nor do they herald the advent of the end times or the fall of the gods.<br /> <br /> All in all, it's a net positive for me and I've enjoyed using and playing Lunars a lot.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-17T21:36:51-07:00","id":1542349,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I think having access to Animal MA Styles while in that animal's form (or a similar one) would have been really cool, even if I do understand that there's a need for balance with these things. It could still have tied in well with the Protean keyword at least.<br /> <br /> I forgot about the magical creature restriction, because I could have sworn that there was a Perception charm that bypassed that, but it turns out God-Body Consumption only grants the Lunar the <i>charms</i> of the character they kill, NOT the shape for spirits which seems... a bit of a letdown, when you primarily would want to use those charms in order to better disguise yourself as that character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26317-critian-caceorte","name":"Critian Caceorte","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-18T00:12:52-07:00","id":1542356,"title":"","content":"My opening salvo: Protean is a kludge that should have been left in 2e. If you keep it, it'll just give you more and more Silver Solar Charms, again and again, forever.<br /> <br /> And from what I scanned from reading through the cascade notes and from the Inkless Pen review, you have an awful lot of Silver Solar when it isn't Silver something else that isn't a Lunar. You have an animal shapeshifting Celestial Exalt that expressed power through said shapeshifting as a concept, and... you abandon that, wholesale, to play Wyrd Sisters for some reason. You write in psychic surgery instead of versatile silk Charms and mother of pearl* production. I clearly do not have the same set of priorities, here.<br /> <br /> If you're perplexed about how to fit shapeshifting into, say, Intelligence or Wits, 1) consider the results and aims of the Charm, and 2) consult the Alchemicals Charm sets (2e preferred, 1e still good, even Time of Tumult's limited samples would have done E:tL a solid), because it has a lot of useful insights on the topic of making the abstract physical. Not all of them translate (brain implants are obviously a no go), but Autochthonians/Alchemicals gets remembered fondly because it tried to plot out a path here. Lunar Charms have a history of just charging headlong into a Solar-esque lack of flavor text, which only works for Solars because they're the fill in the blank heroes, and so Lunars don't usually get beyond &quot;okay, I guess.&quot; That's for 2e, of course; 1e is some shade of &quot;burn it down and start again&quot;.<br /> <br /> That said, the sidebar from 1e, &quot;The Narrow Path&quot;? It wasn't completely off target. I just happen to disagree about how limited in scope relying on shapeshifting actually is. It's like the Exalted equivalent of the guy at the gym fallacy, only contorted around so that people will accept steam!magic implant bullshit doing literally anything, but not that Lunars can use animal shapeshifting to overcome cosmic threats, even though both are just arbitrary effects with a visual hook. Never mind that back in the first mention of magical material armor, design flourishes like fins and spikes on armor were called out as specifically having a function in channeling Essence through the item in question; if it works for armor, why not for armor formed from skin?<br /> <br /> All in all, I would rearrange a lot of where effects show up and would prefer a set where almost everything relies on shapeshifting and anything that Lunars need to have that delves into dreams, the unconscious, etc. could probably get farmed out as a function of moonsilver artifacts.<br /> <br /> I'm not particularly hung up on the lack of shapeshifting into spirits. Not like I'm angry about there being, apparently, lots of Charms to key into Solar bond and none about shocking people with your electric fish-themed bioelectric buildup. Or the absence of Argent Weaver Art. Or that the natural weapons that Charms form aren't made out of moonsilver and capable of striking dematerialized spirits without the need of an additional Charm for it.<br /> <br /> I also understand that they gave Leviathan another Solar mate-related plot tumor to weigh him to the bottom of the ocean. I miss the elements of Leviathan from 1e where he was allowed to not be involved with Solar mate romance plots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude18","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-18T10:59:35-07:00","id":1542376,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542356#post1542356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All in all, I would rearrange a lot of where effects show up and would prefer a set where almost everything relies on shapeshifting and anything that Lunars need to have that delves into dreams, the unconscious, etc. could probably get farmed out as a function of moonsilver artifacts.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't kow, I think the different themes of the Lunar charmset lend themselves well to creating characters closer to mythic (anti)heroes, like Circe, Loki or Sun Wu Kong. Basing all Lunar abilities on shapeshifting could be cool, but more in a Alex Mercer way, that I feel would perhaps not fit as well in the setting? Like it would shift them from Mythic heroes to Superheroes if that makes sense?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-18T12:03:37-07:00","id":1542379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anteros</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542376#post1542376\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't kow, I think the different themes of the Lunar charmset lend themselves well to creating characters closer to mythic (anti)heroes, like Circe, Loki or Sun Wu Kong. Basing all Lunar abilities on shapeshifting could be cool, but more in a Alex Mercer way, that I feel would perhaps not fit as well in the setting? Like it would shift them from Mythic heroes to Superheroes if that makes sense?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Consider the 2e version of the Alchemical Charm, Transcendent Brutality Programming. It gives you a set of claws to use that are an indelible part of the Charm; you can't use the Charm without popping said claws and slashing people. This is a cool effect that's anchored entirely to a physical change, and a good example of how you can make something physical when you're used to just having Solars fart hypnotism out of the aether and cough up Blazing Solar Bolts.<br /> <br /> I'm also having trouble with the issue of Lunars somehow being less mythic for relying on shapeshifting when, again, Alchemicals get off like bandits with their physicality. Lunars relying on shapeshifting could be multi-headed beasts that exhale smoke and flames, striking pillars of Renown that walk on two legs but have the faces of beasts, and champions that can slay the enemies of the gods with their &quot;brazen&quot; moonsilver talons. They would have voices that echo with the roars of great cats and rumble of dragons that could strike terror into their foes and assure their allies of their cause. The realm of the gods would be in their grasp, for they inherit &quot;stepping sideways&quot; and becoming spirit clothing flesh from W:tA, since it primarily involves their form and also fits tales of mythic beasts.<br /> <br /> I'm tired of Lunars material ignoring the <i>Tsarevich Ivan, the Firebird and the Gray Wolf*</i> to go for Baba Yaga. This is particularly galling because the quote used to cite the importance of the latter is about Lunars being... very different from their 3e versions, what with being the raw, unfiltered 1e &quot;destroy all civilization&quot; type and all. I'm tired of hearing about Circe, who never, ever, shapeshifts herself, and could be any God-Blooded sorcerer in Creation with zero changes. I'm tired of people citing Wukong, who can flat out just do everything. No one ever considers &quot;maybe Alchemicals should have Charms that aren't installed&quot;, they shouldn't also say &quot;maybe animal shapeshifting is too limited&quot;.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:9px\">* The wolf in question actually shapeshifts! Even turns into the double of someone else to perpetrate a trick!</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-19T15:49:18-07:00","id":1542439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542356#post1542356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And from what I scanned from reading through the cascade notes and from the Inkless Pen review, you have an awful lot of Silver Solar when it isn't Silver something else that isn't a Lunar.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't doubt that there's a lot of this you'll be dissatisfied with regardless, but I think there are an enormous number of nuances that you're missing if you don't actually read the book itself.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-19T16:18:51-07:00","id":1542440,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anteros</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars#post1542327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm a bit disappointed about how shapeshifted Lunars can't use Martial Arts, even those MA created by Lunars, it feels kinda random and arbitrary, but it's nothing a house rule can't easily fix.</div> </div> </div> </div>There might be a slight balance consideration to it (not least because there's already a somewhat tentative capacity for Lunars to combine their own Charms with Martial Arts in a way almost all other Exalted can't), but I personally think it's mostly a consistency of image thing. The Martial Arts are specific codified fighting styles, and they're channelled through the motions of a human body. A literal snake can't use Snake Style because they don't have any arms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anteros</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also wonder why they lost the ability to take the shape of supernatural creatures.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think through a combination of maintaining a certain focus in them and the fact that once you start introducing lots of other things, animals might look insignificant and become neglected.<br /> <br /> Besides, there's also the matter of what the utility of it is. Part of the point of the animal shapeshifting is that you gain the special capabilities of the animal you turn into. If you turn into a demon, are you supposed to gain all of its powers? Or is it purely cosmetic? If the latter, a lot of the value of it would be lost anyway.<br /> <br /> They were all made to fight monsters of the ancients, but they're still primarily the champions of their patron gods, and have powers expressing that more than being custom made to perform a function.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anteros</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Lastly I believe Lunars still feel more like powerful Terrestrial Exalted than like Celestials. They're strong and versatile, yes, but they don't have the cosmic scope Solars, Abyssals, Infernals, Sidereals or even Getimians have. They don't shape fate, reality or the Chaos at the edge of the world, they don't shake the fundations of Heaven or shatter constellations, nor do they herald the advent of the end times or the fall of the gods.</div> </div> </div> </div>Two things:<br /> <br /> What do the Solars have that actually does all of that?<br /> <br /> There's an Essence 3 Intelligence Charm that can create flaws to break through prophetic effects like the Solar God-King's Shrike, an Essence 3 Stamina Charm to let them inhale storms, and a Perception Charm to let them sense when something that is a threat to one of their Defining Ties is occurring anywhere in the same realm of existence as them.<br /> <br /> Oh hey, I forgot about the Perception Charm to also perceive the weaknesses of another and convert them into a dread prophecy of doom for them.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-19T21:35:41-07:00","id":1542446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542439#post1542439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't doubt that there's a lot of this you'll be dissatisfied with regardless, but I think there are an enormous number of nuances that you're missing if you don't actually read the book itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hit me with the most relevant example you think there is, then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-20T01:36:51-07:00","id":1542448,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542446#post1542446\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hit me with the most relevant example you think there is, then.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have a personal fondness for Charms such as Agile Beast Defence and Bending Before the Storm, which enhance their effects when used against opponents with lower Initiative. It's a small thing in Charms that are at the roots of their cascades, but I think they contribute to this quality of a predatory aspect in the Lunar Essence. <br /> <br /> There are other Charms than those two with that quality, but I can't glean them immediately from the mass just because they use a slightly different phrasing than my word search.<br /> <br /> I personally appreciate the Charms that we got because I have my own attachment to the image of the Lunar where what is animalistic about them is conveyed through a feral mien, an unsettling attitude or way the body doesn't quite move right that makes the human face feel like a facade. I think there's a way to do that which moves it beyond the realm of Silver Solars, and given that I haven't seen that term used by anybody who ever read the Charms I see I'm not the only one who thinks the writers managed it.<br /> <br /> But it's still not the sum total of what the Charms are. There are Charms which describe their effects in terms of the body consisting of flowing, protean flesh. Swarm Charms contain options such as breathing clouds of vermin or turning the surface of their own body into a roiling mass of animal parts as a counterattack. They still have Charms to produce poison or disgorge acid.<br /> <br /> I don't know. I don't see the point for having any highly specific vision of what the powers will be, for any Exalted, because the only way to not be disappointed there is to have a hand in writing them. There's a point at which I think the only reasonable thing to do is look at the conclusions the writers came to from their own vision and creative impulses and trying to find the merits of what they were going for.<br /> <br /> Or if not that, then letting it go rather than spending years trying to find increasingly elaborate terms with which to express one's disdain. &quot;Play Wyrd Sisters for some reason&quot;, what does that even mean?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-20T06:31:48-07:00","id":1542453,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542379#post1542379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Consider the 2e version of the Alchemical Charm, Transcendent Brutality Programming. It gives you a set of claws to use that are an indelible part of the Charm; you can't use the Charm without popping said claws and slashing people. This is a cool effect that's anchored entirely to a physical change, and a good example of how you can make something physical when you're used to just having Solars fart hypnotism out of the aether and cough up Blazing Solar Bolts.<br /> <br /> I'm also having trouble with the issue of Lunars somehow being less mythic for relying on shapeshifting when, again, Alchemicals get off like bandits with their physicality. Lunars relying on shapeshifting could be multi-headed beasts that exhale smoke and flames, striking pillars of Renown that walk on two legs but have the faces of beasts, and champions that can slay the enemies of the gods with their &quot;brazen&quot; moonsilver talons. They would have voices that echo with the roars of great cats and rumble of dragons that could strike terror into their foes and assure their allies of their cause. The realm of the gods would be in their grasp, for they inherit &quot;stepping sideways&quot; and becoming spirit clothing flesh from W:tA, since it primarily involves their form and also fits tales of mythic beasts.<br /> <br /> I'm tired of Lunars material ignoring the <i>Tsarevich Ivan, the Firebird and the Gray Wolf*</i> to go for Baba Yaga. This is particularly galling because the quote used to cite the importance of the latter is about Lunars being... very different from their 3e versions, what with being the raw, unfiltered 1e &quot;destroy all civilization&quot; type and all. I'm tired of hearing about Circe, who never, ever, shapeshifts herself, and could be any God-Blooded sorcerer in Creation with zero changes. I'm tired of people citing Wukong, who can flat out just do everything. No one ever considers &quot;maybe Alchemicals should have Charms that aren't installed&quot;, they shouldn't also say &quot;maybe animal shapeshifting is too limited&quot;.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:9px\">* The wolf in question actually shapeshifts! Even turns into the double of someone else to perpetrate a trick!</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Alchemicals are exactly what I think of when I talk about being more like superheroes than mythic heroes. Alchemical are very superheroic. And that's good! they're amazing! the Alchemicals from the Exalted modern Shard were my second favorite Exalt type after Infernals in 2E.<br /> But they're the magitech heroes of an industrial world, it's kind of a different vibe than Lunars and on purpose. Would it really be such an upgrade to make Lunars the Fleshy Alchemicals to avoid some Silver Solar Charms? <br /> On the other hand, a lot of Lunar Charms are exactly what you're talking about. They just have other options, and it's more or less possible to just not take the Charms one doesn't like. More options are always better than less in my book.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-20T07:26:44-07:00","id":1542454,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542379#post1542379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Consider the 2e version of the Alchemical Charm, Transcendent Brutality Programming. It gives you a set of claws to use that are an indelible part of the Charm; you can't use the Charm without popping said claws and slashing people. This is a cool effect that's anchored entirely to a physical change, and a good example of how you can make something physical when you're used to just having Solars fart hypnotism out of the aether and cough up Blazing Solar Bolts.<br /> <br /> I'm also having trouble with the issue of Lunars somehow being less mythic for relying on shapeshifting when, again, Alchemicals get off like bandits with their physicality. Lunars relying on shapeshifting could be multi-headed beasts that exhale smoke and flames, striking pillars of Renown that walk on two legs but have the faces of beasts, and champions that can slay the enemies of the gods with their &quot;brazen&quot; moonsilver talons. They would have voices that echo with the roars of great cats and rumble of dragons that could strike terror into their foes and assure their allies of their cause. The realm of the gods would be in their grasp, for they inherit &quot;stepping sideways&quot; and becoming spirit clothing flesh from W:tA, since it primarily involves their form and also fits tales of mythic beasts.<br /> <br /> I'm tired of Lunars material ignoring the <i>Tsarevich Ivan, the Firebird and the Gray Wolf*</i> to go for Baba Yaga. This is particularly galling because the quote used to cite the importance of the latter is about Lunars being... very different from their 3e versions, what with being the raw, unfiltered 1e &quot;destroy all civilization&quot; type and all. I'm tired of hearing about Circe, who never, ever, shapeshifts herself, and could be any God-Blooded sorcerer in Creation with zero changes. I'm tired of people citing Wukong, who can flat out just do everything. No one ever considers &quot;maybe Alchemicals should have Charms that aren't installed&quot;, they shouldn't also say &quot;maybe animal shapeshifting is too limited&quot;.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:9px\">* The wolf in question actually shapeshifts! Even turns into the double of someone else to perpetrate a trick!</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Alchemicals are exactly what I think of when I talk about being more like superheroes than mythic heroes. Alchemical are very superheroic. And that's good! they're amazing! the Alchemicals from the Exalted modern Shard were my second favorite Exalt type after Infernals in 2E.<br /> But they're the magitech heroes of an industrial world, it's kind of a different vibe than Lunars and on purpose. Would it really be such an upgrade to make Lunars the Fleshy Alchemicals to avoid some Silver Solar Charms? <br /> On the other hand, a lot of Lunar Charms are exactly what you're talking about. They just have other options, and it's more or less possible to just not take the Charms one doesn't like. More options are always better than less in my book.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-20T09:49:04-07:00","id":1542459,"title":"","content":"I think of options as being like the toppings of a pizza. A good pizza assembles a particular collection of complementary and cohesive toppings that come together to make a particular experience. In some cases, the most effective cohesion comes from being outright limited with your selection.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200bOne has not devised the best pizza ever if it piles as many toppings as possible on there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-20T10:08:21-07:00","id":1542460,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have a personal fondness for Charms such as Agile Beast Defence and Bending Before the Storm, which enhance their effects when used against opponents with lower Initiative. It's a small thing in Charms that are at the roots of their cascades, but I think they contribute to this quality of a predatory aspect in the Lunar Essence.<br /> <br /> There are other Charms than those two with that quality, but I can't glean them immediately from the mass just because they use a slightly different phrasing than my word search.<br /> <br /> I personally appreciate the Charms that we got because I have my own attachment to the image of the Lunar where what is animalistic about them is conveyed through a feral mien, an unsettling attitude or way the body doesn't quite move right that makes the human face feel like a facade. I think there's a way to do that which moves it beyond the realm of Silver Solars, and given that I haven't seen that term used by anybody who ever read the Charms I see I'm not the only one who thinks the writers managed it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not moving quite right seems like it would work for the base of the Charm tree, but not for something deep into a tree and requiring high Attributes and Essence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But it's still not the sum total of what the Charms are. There are Charms which describe their effects in terms of the body consisting of flowing, protean flesh. </div> </div> </div>Flowing, protean flesh needs definition for a specific effect, even for dodges. Saying that a Charm works via flowing, protean flesh it like saying that it works by using Essence. Plus, that imagery is sailing close to 2e chimerae.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Swarm Charms contain options such as breathing clouds of vermin or turning the surface of their own body into a roiling mass of animal parts as a counterattack. They still have Charms to produce poison or disgorge acid. </div> </div> </div>At that point we enter a vast difference of opinion about which Attribute those should fall under. I think that producing substances should fall under Intelligence, but the book wants to go with Stamina. But, if you're going by mythic associations, why would you make it so that the super-tough characters are the ones doing trickster-grade stuff? Poison in myth has a whole host of connotations in its use that would seem to eschew putting it in the hands of the most direct characters there are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know. I don't see the point for having any highly specific vision of what the powers will be, for any Exalted, because the only way to not be disappointed there is to have a hand in writing them. There's a point at which I think the only reasonable thing to do is look at the conclusions the writers came to from their own vision and creative impulses and trying to find the merits of what they were going for. </div> </div> </div>As noted in my first post, I can't really get my enthusiasm around it. You can<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or if not that, then letting it go rather than spending years trying to find increasingly elaborate terms with which to express one's disdain. &quot;Play Wyrd Sisters for some reason&quot;, what does that even mean? </div> </div> </div>I did say that even after a long absence, I'd be back. Most every other game tends to affix animal shapeshifting powers to a particular spellcasting archetype; Lunars is kind of an outlier in not really doing that. And I'm pretty sure you know which one it is that was imposing on that, even with that obtuse question where you pretend to not recall past conversations about it.<br /> <br /> I've been away for an awfully long time, too. During that period, I was, in fact, mostly not thinking about Lunars. There were a lot of other things to consider, matters in day to day life that thankfully took my away from it. You're the one who decided to respond to an off-hand, jokey comment in a W:tA thread and spark my present change in attitude, which has driven me to write (at present) 63 pages of purely shapeshifting-based 3e Charms for a bootleg set. Don't piss down my back and tell me it's raining, Isator.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anteros</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542453#post1542453\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Alchemicals are exactly what I think of when I talk about being more like superheroes than mythic heroes. Alchemical are very superheroic. And that's good! they're amazing! the Alchemicals from the Exalted modern Shard were my second favorite Exalt type after Infernals in 2E.</div> </div> </div> </div>Modern Alchemicals were almost a completely different Exalt. They got moved from artificial people made vaguely like the Golem to literally The Guyver. Having a set of claws that ravages the mind, in contrast, doesn't seem a whole lot like that, and I've found it easy to approach things that way (I commandeered part of the name of Feral Smile Tactic for scaring the shit out people with a grin full of sharp fangs such that they reconsidered their allegiances and views, but I had originally given that the name What Big Teeth You Have).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But they're the magitech heroes of an industrial world, it's kind of a different vibe than Lunars and on purpose. Would it really be such an upgrade to make Lunars the Fleshy Alchemicals to avoid some Silver Solar Charms? </div> </div> </div>You do realize that you're talking to the person who made a total conversion of Alchemical Charms to Lunar ones many, many times since 2005, right? And I may have plans to do it again after the Alchemicals manuscript drops later this year or early next. I don't use everything, necessarily, but I've found it an interesting challenge, to the point where I obviously do think that it's doable and preferable, or else I wouldn't have spent so much time arguing that lessons of Attribute-based Charms from the Alchemicals could really, really help ground Lunar Charms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the other hand, a lot of Lunar Charms are exactly what you're talking about. They just have other options, and it's more or less possible to just not take the Charms one doesn't like. More options are always better than less in my book. </div> </div> </div>The &quot;options&quot; for Intelligence and Wits feel like slaps in the face. The set is telling me that unless I make a character a witch, they shouldn't favor them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-27T09:58:34-07:00","id":1542782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542460#post1542460\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not moving quite right seems like it would work for the base of the Charm tree, but not for something deep into a tree and requiring high Attributes and Essence.</div> </div> </div> </div>The two Charms that I've described by name <i>are</i> that, though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Saur Ops Specialist]<br /> Flowing, protean flesh needs definition for a specific effect, even for dodges. Saying that a Charm works via flowing, protean flesh it like saying that it works by using Essence. </div> </div> </div>I'm a little unsure of whether this is being said as a criticism in terms of &quot;its unfulfilling because it's only for the sake of a specific effect&quot; or &quot;it's unfulfilling because it doesn't do enough to achieve a specific effect&quot;.<br /> <br /> If it was the former, I'd just point out that's simply how Charms work. They're almost always focused towards a particular result, rather than opening something to be freeform.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">At that point we enter a vast difference of opinion about which Attribute those should fall under. I think that producing substances should fall under Intelligence, but the book wants to go with Stamina. But, if you're going by mythic associations, why would you make it so that the super-tough characters are the ones doing trickster-grade stuff? Poison in myth has a whole host of connotations in its use that would seem to eschew putting it in the hands of the most direct characters there are.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the premise of a certain kind of manipulation of bodily functions and secretions reasonably falls under Stamina among the choices for Attributes. I think it also falls under the headers of giving Attributes variety (both in service to the larger premise of Lunar versatility and because limited Attributes means they ought to have a few tricks to make them worthwhile), and helps broaden the range of possibilities for Full Moon Caste characters. Besides, it's adaptation of Second Edition Charms that would be popular.<br /> <br /> That said, I'm looking at some of the Lunar Intelligence crafting Charms, and between some elements already present and some of the ways it would resonate with some Third Edition Lunar aesthetics, I think there's certainly space for homebrewing additions to that cascade that would be focused on some elements of poison. I don't think its outright production as such (which would be just a normal application of a Craft action) so much as things that could be done with it that would go beyond the norm. I'm thinking of the deadly robe created by Medea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most every other game tends to affix animal shapeshifting powers to a particular spellcasting archetype; Lunars is kind of an outlier in not really doing that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is this a statement that other games attach shapeshifting to more of an Earthsea style wizard rather than the particular kind of archetype that Fangs at the Gate went with?<br /> <br /> If it is, I don't agree with that. I think even as far as other Onyx Path games go, one would get to both version of Mage in which the Sphere/Arcanum for a person shapeshifting into an animal falls under the Verbena Tradition and Thyrsus Path, both of which seem more like Lunar forms of magical outsider than other options. And those ideas for mages are themselves drawing on other literary precedents and mythical motifs.<br /> <br /> Or is this about &quot;shamans&quot;? That's still part of the Lunar composition, but I think the emphasis has shifted that away from being about magic and more into the realms of community and religious function, because the latter is more about what shamans are and the former is kind of appropriative in a way that the books were very specifically trying to get away from, even challenge. That's part of why there was a revision to make the Keyword for alternate Attributes based on spirit shape into &quot;Iconic&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're the one who decided to respond to an off-hand, jokey comment in a W:tA thread</div> </div> </div> </div>It didn't strike me as a &quot;ha ha&quot; kind of joke, and for being in a conversation that otherwise had nothing to do with Lunars and among people who might not have read the book, I felt it misrepresented.<br /> <br /> I would remind that the only thing I said there was to dispute the stated premise and point out that there were lots of Charms concerned with being empowered by straight up shapeshifting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The &quot;options&quot; for Intelligence and Wits feel like slaps in the face. The set is telling me that unless I make a character a witch, they shouldn't favor them.</div> </div> </div> </div>You should favour Intelligence if you want to be a storyteller and teacher (often teaching through storytelling). A crafter and healer (including the forms of healing that allow you to manipulate flesh in forms beyond the capacity of normal medicine). Somebody who cows and commands and binds spirits. It's also where one-third of their warfare Charms go, since Intelligence is one of the Attributes made to use command actions.<br /> <br /> (As is Appearance, and there are some scary things under Lunar Appearance for warfare).<br /> <br /> Wits has the powers to let a Lunar be a dedicated ally or companion or protector, by means of which they have their own take on creating alternate personalities. It's where they empower and uprise and coordinate with their familiars (which can culminate in things like giving them human intelligence and initiating them into sorcery). It lets them find their way through unfamiliar lands, have prophetic insight into potential obstacles, obscure and misdirect their path from pursuers. It's the Attribute to steal things, to hide things, to store things Elsewhere (for which they have broader range than Solars) or in containers too small to hold them (which has the advantage of making them accessible to allies), to retroactively create traps or be in possession of circumstantially useful minor wonders. And it's where half of their territory Charms are (the half that follow the general theme of &quot;yeah, don't follow them in there&quot;).<br /> <br /> Maybe all of that might say &quot;be a witch&quot; to somebody, but they're hardly limited options or lack for other character archetypes to be. Indeed, all being under a single Attribute, they allow for one to be many archetypes simultaneously. A person can favour Intelligence and be a witch, a surgeon, a loremaster, and a guerilla general all at once.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-28T20:48:51-07:00","id":1542851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542782#post1542782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The two Charms that I've described by name <i>are</i> that, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>So close, yet the rest of our talk is with knives.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm a little unsure of whether this is being said as a criticism in terms of &quot;its unfulfilling because it's only for the sake of a specific effect&quot; or &quot;it's unfulfilling because it doesn't do enough to achieve a specific effect&quot;.<br /> <br /> If it was the former, I'd just point out that's simply how Charms work. They're almost always focused towards a particular result, rather than opening something to be freeform. </div> </div> </div>Why didn't you assume the latter?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the premise of a certain kind of manipulation of bodily functions and secretions reasonably falls under Stamina among the choices for Attributes. I think it also falls under the headers of giving Attributes variety (both in service to the larger premise of Lunar versatility and because limited Attributes means they ought to have a few tricks to make them worthwhile), and helps broaden the range of possibilities for Full Moon Caste characters. </div> </div> </div>But why? Direct, stand-up combatants are not the first in line for going laterally with toxins, and most of animals that have those traits probably aren't going to be high Stamina types.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Besides, it's adaptation of Second Edition Charms that would be popular. </div> </div> </div>That shouldn't enter into it. In fact, just re-doing 2e is one of the issues that I brought up in my last post.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, I'm looking at some of the Lunar Intelligence crafting Charms, and between some elements already present and some of the ways it would resonate with some Third Edition Lunar aesthetics, I think there's certainly space for homebrewing additions to that cascade that would be focused on some elements of poison. I don't think its outright production as such (which would be just a normal application of a Craft action) so much as things that could be done with it that would go beyond the norm. I'm thinking of the deadly robe created by Medea. </div> </div> </div>You would.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is this a statement that other games attach shapeshifting to more of an Earthsea style wizard rather than the particular kind of archetype that Fangs at the Gate went with?<br /> <br /> If it is, I don't agree with that. I think even as far as other Onyx Path games go, one would get to both version of Mage in which the Sphere/Arcanum for a person shapeshifting into an animal falls under the Verbena Tradition and Thyrsus Path, both of which seem more like Lunar forms of magical outsider than other options. And those ideas for mages are themselves drawing on other literary precedents and mythical motifs.<br /> <br /> Or is this about &quot;shamans&quot;? That's still part of the Lunar composition, but I think the emphasis has shifted that away from being about magic and more into the realms of community and religious function, because the latter is more about what shamans are and the former is kind of appropriative in a way that the books were very specifically trying to get away from, even challenge. That's part of why there was a revision to make the Keyword for alternate Attributes based on spirit shape into &quot;Iconic&quot;. </div> </div> </div>All of the above, plus &quot;druids&quot;, the most pervasive fucking archetype that fantasy trpgs use on account of the ampersand game. How did that not occur to you even though you brought up the Verbena?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It didn't strike me as a &quot;ha ha&quot; kind of joke </div> </div> </div>Then again, it's fairly obvious that neither of us get each other, so keep that in mind for the future.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and for being in a conversation that otherwise had nothing to do with Lunars and among people who might not have read the book, I felt it misrepresented. </div> </div> </div>Polish those apples!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I would remind that the only thing I said there was to dispute the stated premise and point out that there were lots of Charms concerned with being empowered by straight up shapeshifting. </div> </div> </div>You said that there were a lot of Protean Charms, and I'm not really buying that those two are the same things. One is actually establishing a strong look for an effect; the other is just throwing up its hands and saying &quot;I guess you can just turn into another form and get a benny, if you have one that applies. If not, you're kind of SOL.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You should favour Intelligence if you want to be a storyteller and teacher (often teaching through storytelling). </div> </div> </div>But why not Charisma and Manipulation?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A crafter and healer (including the forms of healing that allow you to manipulate flesh in forms beyond the capacity of normal medicine). Somebody who cows and commands and binds spirits. It's also where one-third of their warfare Charms go, since Intelligence is one of the Attributes made to use command actions. </div> </div> </div>Over half the command Int tree, according to the Charm cascades, is available through Stamina if you have any spirit shape that lives and operates in a group. That's a pretty big list (being social, as it turns out, is actually quite beneficial as far as strategies go), and also as a side, kind of puts holes in the themes of the trees, if you were going for them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (As is Appearance, and there are some scary things under Lunar Appearance for warfare). </div> </div> </div>I mean, I should <i>hope so</i>. Half of Appearance is Hideous, and Appearance is one of the easiest Attributes to work shapeshifting into.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Wits has the powers to let a Lunar be a dedicated ally or companion or protector, by means of which they have their own take on creating alternate personalities. </div> </div> </div>It gives them a shadow. That seems really, <i>really</i> Abyssal, and where it isn't Abyssal, it's almost certainly way more Liminal or Umbral than Lunar.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's where they empower and uprise and coordinate with their familiars (which can culminate in things like giving them human intelligence and initiating them into sorcery). </div> </div> </div>The Charm to give them sorcery is called Witch Beast Ascendancy. Hell, the Int tree has references to witches at the top and bottom, too. What else could the takeaway be for those Attributes?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It lets them find their way through unfamiliar lands, have prophetic insight into potential obstacles, obscure and misdirect their path from pursuers. It's the Attribute to steal things, to hide things, to store things Elsewhere (for which they have broader range than Solars) or in containers too small to hold them (which has the advantage of making them accessible to allies), to retroactively create traps or be in possession of circumstantially useful minor wonders. And it's where half of their territory Charms are (the half that follow the general theme of &quot;yeah, don't follow them in there&quot;). </div> </div> </div>Another apparently unpopular opinion: Territory, much like Fury, should be dispersed through the entire set, not loaded into one particular part.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Maybe all of that might say &quot;be a witch&quot; to somebody, but they're hardly limited options or lack for other character archetypes to be. Indeed, all being under a single Attribute, they allow for one to be many archetypes simultaneously. A person can favour Intelligence and be a witch, a surgeon, a loremaster, and a guerilla general all at once. </div> </div> </div>Well, as long as we're talking about witch archetypes, let's talk about the Sidereals being way more fitting!<br /> <br /> - Discounted as being more fragile, required to be tricky way more often than &quot;look at all my health levels!&quot; Lunars who can get two purchases of their Charm to practically spill HLs all over the place and still have plenty to spare even if they didn't put any dots in Stamina.<br /> - Associated with activities that aren't &quot;warrior fare&quot; even when taking combat abilities (Archery is one of the best Abilities for healing!). Note that every Sidereal had to take Craft to literally weave fate for two editions as part of their job, possibly three as well (didn't back, don't know)<br /> - Corollary to this, their Charms are incredibly weird and dictated by fate-related shenanigans. Yet another motif that shows up among various witches in fiction, along with a general preoccupation with what's ordained and what's in prophecy.<br /> - Because of their limited Charm set, they're big into sorcery if they aren't into martial arts.<br /> - Can turn into a corvid, and also have spiritual familiars in large numbers, so if you wanted someone who was already kind of shapeshifting but not all the way and had some ties to animal gods, there you go!<br /> - Access to heavenly libraries, so you have facts casually falling out of your pockets, for the knowledgeable sage portion.<br /> - Starmetal has all the associations of witchcraft without arbitrarily adding &quot;and witchcraft&quot; as an afterthought like with moonsilver.<br /> - Being on the top of the heap but mostly in secret brings to mind Monstress's Cumaea, the witch-nuns. Don't go assuming that witch means &quot;part of an unsupported rebellion&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-01T07:07:43-07:00","id":1542950,"title":"","content":"Before I look at anything else, I would comment that the main reason why I think any comparisons to Alchemicals are going down the wrong path is that a big reason why those Exalted are able to get away with the fact that the actual substance of their Charms is often direct and covers pretty much all bases is that the element of resembling prosthetics or plug ins isn't just aesthetic, it's integrated into the very concept that Alchemicals can only access a limited number of their Charms at once, and need to go through a reformatting process to do things differently.<br /> <br /> I don't think there's anything Lunars could do that would be comparable that would not end up just trying to chase after something established for other Exalted (or even the Fair Folk) that is less theme cohesive.<br /> <br /> A sense of versatility and adaptability is not integral to the Lunar concept, but it's got a lot of establishment, enough to validate a decision to largely not design them towards an idea that use of their powers across the board requires shifting into a particular form and not being able to readily assume others.<br /> <br /> And if they didn't have that limitation, then its just giving them a large suite of Charms whose only sense of character is cosmetic, which I think is out of place for the general design philosophy of Exalted. For all Exalted, I think the strongest way to express personality and the kind of narrative they empower is in the functions of the Charms themselves, where the cosmetic elements are secondary (hence why there are many Terrestrial Charms with no overt elemental manifestation). I think the guy who wrote Keychain of Creation put it best in how this is largely a matter of that being most of the level at which things are engaged with and feel most tactile, because there's no genuine visual element to any of it.<br /> <br /> I think the game has a sturdy and well-established foundation of how most of what makes any of the Exalted evocative is in the stuff that takes place under the hood. Like how the Solar Exalted have a lot of things to represent their brand of being a superlative weapons fighter that function solely through dice mechanics that are not apparent to any onlookers in character. <br /> <br /> I regard that as the ultimate failing of Lunar Charms in past Editions; one way or another, they were too function based, rather than finding something comparable to how a Dragon Blooded can be athletic in a way that is evocative of fire even when they're not actually using flame jets to propel themselves. Third Edition was successful both at capturing the sense of what the Lunar would be evocative of and mechanics that then do that (while still creating positive feedback for fully shapeshifting into forms that align with what is being evoked).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-01T10:17:46-07:00","id":1542958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1542950#post1542950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Before I look at anything else, I would comment that the main reason why I think any comparisons to Alchemicals are going down the wrong path is that a big reason why those Exalted are able to get away with the fact that the actual substance of their Charms is often direct and covers pretty much all bases is that the element of resembling prosthetics or plug ins isn't just aesthetic, it's integrated into the very concept that Alchemicals can only access a limited number of their Charms at once, and need to go through a reformatting process to do things differently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it's kind of difficult to make your arm two things at once. Setting up a system of mutually exclusive animal shapeshifting tricks based on what they're altering doesn't seem like it would be that difficult, and still manages to be difficult from Alchemicals because they're not rushing back to base to re-tool. If your natural weapons get keyed into construction or reaching into minds through wounds, they'll be less effective at tearing enemies apart. Forming up the rippers makes subtler tools less effective, because why bother with delicate fangs that can deliver venom even without an apparently serious injury if your quarry is already missing chunks of their body?<br /> <br /> It takes a Keyword or two.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't think there's anything Lunars could do that would be comparable that would not end up just trying to chase after something established for other Exalted (or even the Fair Folk) that is less theme cohesive. </div> </div> </div>As opposed to animal shapeshifters having next to no Charms that tap into direct shapeshifting? Protean doesn't do this; it indirectly incentivizes shapeshifting depending on whether or not you have the form. It's empty. It's a lingering patch from the bland as hell 2e Charm set, stretched over a different wound.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A sense of versatility and adaptability is not integral to the Lunar concept, but it's got a lot of establishment, enough to validate a decision to largely not design them towards an idea that use of their powers across the board requires shifting into a particular form and not being able to readily assume others. </div> </div> </div>And it's absolutely going to fall into the same trap if you think of these concepts first. Approaching this from a vantage point primarily of getting effects in is going to get you very close to where Solars are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And if they didn't have that limitation, then its just giving them a large suite of Charms whose only sense of character is cosmetic, which I think is out of place for the general design philosophy of Exalted. For all Exalted, I think the strongest way to express personality and the kind of narrative they empower is in the functions of the Charms themselves, where the cosmetic elements are secondary (hence why there are many Terrestrial Charms with no overt elemental manifestation). I think the guy who wrote Keychain of Creation put it best in how this is largely a matter of that being most of the level at which things are engaged with and feel most tactile, because there's no genuine visual element to any of it. </div> </div> </div>Dragon-Blooded still cram elementally-flavored effects down even into their Excellencies. It's enough to get people on board with actually having to buy them in 3e.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, the narrative of the Lunars is that they are animal shapeshifters. Why else would you be here if not for that? Because if you're looking for adaptability and versatility in a character, well, Solars exist. Exigents of all kinds exist, and are encouraged to just take bits of existing Charm sets if it fits. Seems like Lunars would be a great place to show what that is, for themselves and the animal god Exigents.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the game has a sturdy and well-established foundation of how most of what makes any of the Exalted evocative is in the stuff that takes place under the hood. Like how the Solar Exalted have a lot of things to represent their brand of being a superlative weapons fighter that function solely through dice mechanics that are not apparent to any onlookers in character. </div> </div> </div>You keep going back to them, even though we've established that they're an aberration in thematics; a near-blank slate to project onto, just as long as you're not looking for something monstrous or too otherworldly. Rerolling 1s and saying &quot;you didn't see that!&quot; isn't a theme, it's what you do for thing you're really good at, and Solars are blandly good at everything because the only other thing they have is Sunny Delight.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I regard that as the ultimate failing of Lunar Charms in past Editions; one way or another, they were too function based, rather than finding something comparable to how a Dragon Blooded can be athletic in a way that is evocative of fire even when they're not actually using flame jets to propel themselves. Third Edition was successful both at capturing the sense of what the Lunar would be evocative of and mechanics that then do that (while still creating positive feedback for fully shapeshifting into forms that align with what is being evoked). </div> </div> </div>That's hilarious, because the Lunar Charms of past editions didn't do what I'm proposing, either. There are Silver Solar effects by the boatload in 1e, with occasional bits of interesting flavor that get ridiculous power clipping because of the black hole of power that is DBT warping the Charm set out from the center. 2e is just bland all around, since it pulled all of its themes out and locked them in a Knack box, before repeating the Silver Solar problem, but worse. 3e didn't break away from this, by any means; it just kept following the same path down the road a bit, and meekly suggests that you'd probably like to shapeshift, it'll give you a carrot like you're a Sidereal looking for an appropriate place for an astrological College representation (if you have it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-02T12:30:56-07:00","id":1543023,"title":"","content":"You know, I'm looking back over some of the Charms that more directly involve bodily modifications. Things like the Charm for breathing underwater entailing the Lunar developing gills or growing additional lungs. The Charm for climbing sheer surfaces by moulding their body to adhere to even the smallest forms of purchase. Adder Fang Method is described specifically in terms of growing skin glands or venom ducts to convey their poison. And Kraken-Arm Lash is something to extend a limb out to medium range to grapple somebody. Plus some others that describe the mechanism for action in terms of some change but leave the specifics to the taste and imagination of the player.<br /> <br /> It's my preference that not all Charms be that by default, but I also see that as the kind of thing that creates a firm basis for <i>stunting</i> Charms that aren't like that by default to entail some additional physical element.<br /> <br /> It wouldn't cover everything. It's still not in the Lunar wheelhouse to have a Charm to express some power just because it's a novel trait possessed by an animal. On the other hand, as I said back in the Electric Lunar thread, something as specific as wanting eel electrocution already strikes me as more of a Supernatural Merit by way of mutation anyway, and Perfected Hybrid Interaction is an Essence 2 Charm (no Attribute) that allows a Lunar to acquire mutations from outside of their spirit shape. It's more limited than Hybrid Body Transformation, only grants four dots per purchase and can only be purchased (Essence) times, but it's still an inroad provided for them to possess such capabilities at will that is not shared by other Exalted (where you either have to purchase them at character creation or use external sources of magic that are potentially hazardous to acquire such Merits).<br /> <br /> That's also where Lunar creation of spider silk goes; Spinnerets is a three-dot Merit for generating lines of silk that function as exceptional equipment for just about everything somebody could think to use such for (including swinging around like Spider-Man). These things weren't eliminated entirely, there was just a revaluation of what the most cohesive way for them to be accessed by Lunars was.<br /> <br /> Oh, actually, the Charm Sharing Luna's Gifts also allows a Lunar to grant themselves mutations based on shapes they possess outside of the spirit shape for only three motes a pop (each use of the Charm can grant up to five Merit dots), plus experience debt. It differs from Perfected Hybrid Interaction in that while that Charm allows one to shapeshift in and out of the mutations for one mote per Merit dot, Sharing Luna's Gifts is permanent. In any case, it is an Essence 1 Charm.<br /> <br /> While being Merits puts some things below the level of what Charms are capable of, I think the Third Edition approach to Merits in general and Supernatural Merits in particular makes the Lunar capacity to harness them a lot more freely than other Exalted thus far a worthwhile part of their repertoire. <br /> <br /> I'm wondering about Lunar Charms that key specifically off of mutations... I don't think they would be suitable for publication, because accounting for the range of Merits that might be a prerequisite would be really messy and I think they'd have awkward interactions for powers to remove mutations, but I think they can be viable as a space for Lunar homebrew.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-02T14:15:03-07:00","id":1543027,"title":"","content":"My advocacy for the Protean Keyword is straightforward and twofold.<br /> <br /> One is that it creates an incentive for a particular kind of Lunar aesthetic, in which continuous shifting through numerous full animal shapes is emphasized. It also contributes to a particular tone to their social interactions; there are Protean Charms for human shapes to provide bonuses towards people with Intimacies to the original identities. Indeed, there are incentives to perform interaction in animal shapes as well, for when people have Intimacies (particular cultural) towards those. The Lunar presence in such scenes is distinguished if they do it in the shape of a locally venerated beast.<br /> <br /> The other reason is that Second Edition's introduction of the idea of an innate capacity to acquire and assume shapes, unrelated to Charms, is apparently something Third Edition developers saw value in to contribute for their distinct character and provide for unique mechanical advantages. The problem is that it can ultimately be superceded by their Charms; it's all well and good to turn into a tyrant lizard for extra strength, but why do that once you have a Charm to provide mythic feats of strength? By making it that such mythic feats become enhanced when performed in a complementary shape, the innate shapeshifting is able to keep its relevance as the Lunar grows, rather than being a quaint thing that they outgrow.<br /> <br /> It may not be to a given person's taste, and they may even think the execution leaves something to be desired. But I think to dismiss it as shallow does a disservice to the people who directed it from the top.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 10-02-2024, 08:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-02T15:20:38-07:00","id":1543031,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1543023#post1543023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know, I'm looking back over some of the Charms that more directly involve bodily modifications. Things like the Charm for breathing underwater entailing the Lunar developing gills or growing additional lungs. The Charm for climbing sheer surfaces by moulding their body to adhere to even the smallest forms of purchase. Adder Fang Method is described specifically in terms of growing skin glands or venom ducts to convey their poison. And Kraken-Arm Lash is something to extend a limb out to medium range to grapple somebody. Plus some others that describe the mechanism for action in terms of some change but leave the specifics to the taste and imagination of the player.<br /> <br /> It's my preference that not all Charms be that by default, but I also see that as the kind of thing that creates a firm basis for <i>stunting</i> Charms that aren't like that by default to entail some additional physical element.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mention of stunting being required reminds me of how taxing and pointless the Excellencies seem now. Jump through the hoops to get your real dice pool! At this point, I'd prefer just appending success converters from 1e, standardized at 1 mote per die replaced with a success. I don't want to be bothered with requiring stunts to do something. Stunts are something extra.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It wouldn't cover everything. It's still not in the Lunar wheelhouse to have a Charm to express some power just because it's a novel trait possessed by an animal. On the other hand, as I said back in the Electric Lunar thread, something as specific as wanting eel electrocution already strikes me as more of a Supernatural Merit by way of mutation anyway, and Perfected Hybrid Interaction is an Essence 2 Charm (no Attribute) that allows a Lunar to acquire mutations from outside of their spirit shape. It's more limited than Hybrid Body Transformation, only grants four dots per purchase and can only be purchased (Essence) times, but it's still an inroad provided for them to possess such capabilities at will that is not shared by other Exalted (where you either have to purchase them at character creation or use external sources of magic that are potentially hazardous to acquire such Merits).<br /> <br /> That's also where Lunar creation of spider silk goes; Spinnerets is a three-dot Merit for generating lines of silk that function as exceptional equipment for just about everything somebody could think to use such for (including swinging around like Spider-Man). These things weren't eliminated entirely, there was just a revaluation of what the most cohesive way for them to be accessed by Lunars was.<br /> <br /> Oh, actually, the Charm Sharing Luna's Gifts also allows a Lunar to grant themselves mutations based on shapes they possess outside of the spirit shape for only three motes a pop (each use of the Charm can grant up to five Merit dots), plus experience debt. It differs from Perfected Hybrid Interaction in that while that Charm allows one to shapeshift in and out of the mutations for one mote per Merit dot, Sharing Luna's Gifts is permanent. In any case, it is an Essence 1 Charm.<br /> <br /> While being Merits puts some things below the level of what Charms are capable of, I think the Third Edition approach to Merits in general and Supernatural Merits in particular makes the Lunar capacity to harness them a lot more freely than other Exalted thus far a worthwhile part of their repertoire.<br /> <br /> I'm wondering about Lunar Charms that key specifically off of mutations... I don't think they would be suitable for publication, because accounting for the range of Merits that might be a prerequisite would be really messy and I think they'd have awkward interactions for powers to remove mutations, but I think they can be viable as a space for Lunar homebrew. </div> </div> </div>Having Lunars work via mutations is also a misstep from my POV. Sure, DBT Gifts took extra work to describe, but even if you took away the bonkers Attribute boosts, the Lunars in hybrid form weren't the same as J. Random Beastfolk. Oh, you can do a couple extra dice of damage with your claws? Mine are equal to a daiklave. You can spit venom? I can exhale a corrosive cloud that dissolves Dragon-Blooded in the blink of an eye. Expand their roles, have them slot in where you would use mutation-gaining effects, and don't settle.<br /> <br /> With mutations, especially since mutations largely got toned way down for 2e, Lunars lost their edge here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My advocacy for the Protean Keyword is straightforward and twofold.<br /> <br /> One is that it creates an incentive for a particular kind of Lunar aesthetic, in which continuous shifting through numerous full animal shapes is emphasized. </div> </div> </div>I feel that circumstances alone could drive this, and that this is where stunting should be. Protean is basically taking the place of stunts for specific shapes. You know how the game abhors a white room? You're going to be changing based on the battlegrounds. Being a tyrant lizard is wholly impractical in a forest or jungle, except for a few clearings here and there. Small shapes can take advantage of an entire world under the notice of most human-sized beings.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also contributes to a particular tone to their social interactions; there are Protean Charms for human shapes to provide bonuses towards people with Intimacies to the original identities. </div> </div> </div>And that, too, seems like a benefit of the shape alone. The Intimacy toward a shape is probably part of why you got it in the first place. The game after that is making sure that they don't find out you're not who they think you are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Indeed, there are incentives to perform interaction in animal shapes as well, for when people have Intimacies (particular cultural) towards those. The Lunar presence in such scenes is distinguished if they do it in the shape of a locally venerated beast. </div> </div> </div>That, also, seems like it's a general cultural thing that's a bit external and circumstantial for a Charm. The social influence rules seem like already had it covered. Lunars shouldn't just be &quot;Solar ability to do something with culture or spirits, but with animals&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The other reason is that Second Edition's introduction of the idea of an innate capacity to acquire and assume shapes, unrelated to Charms, </div> </div> </div>Except when they had to get Knacks, aka Charms with extra steps, to do it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> is apparently something Third Edition developers saw value in to contribute for their distinct character and provide for unique mechanical advantages. The problem is that it can ultimately be superceded by their Charms; it's all well and good to turn into a tyrant lizard for extra strength, but why do that once you have a Charm to provide mythic feats of strength? By making it that such mythic feats become enhanced when performed in a complementary shape, the innate shapeshifting is able to keep its relevance as the Lunar grows, rather than being a quaint thing that they outgrow. </div> </div> </div>That's why you build their Charms to not just supercede them. I'm pretty sure it doesn't require a Keyword to do it, seeing as how every Charm hits that kind of wall for everyone and every type of Exalted has to consider something else to do when you've already maxed out feats of Strength. Kind of like debilitating effects, there's no need for a mechanical call out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It may not be to a given person's taste, and they may even think the execution leaves something to be desired. But I think to dismiss it as shallow does a disservice to the people who directed it from the top. </div> </div> </div>It's shallow and also unnecessary. Beast forms already have benefits that can be honed further with latent abilities; lining those up is a good reason to cycle between shapes on its own. Lunar Charms would give the oomph to make them turn magically invisible because their body lets light through or more limbs than any animal in their library has and that have more mechanical weight than a mutation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-04T05:48:17-07:00","id":1543093,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1543031#post1543031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And that, too, seems like a benefit of the shape alone. The Intimacy toward a shape is probably part of why you got it in the first place. The game after that is making sure that they don't find out you're not who they think you are.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you may misunderstand. I'm not saying there are Charms to let you use an Intimacy, I'm saying that there are Charms with effects that key off an Intimacy being present for the shape's true identity.<br /> <br /> Notably, that kind of magic works whether the person knows you're the real deal or not.<br /> <br /> Still, you really seem to go back and forth on whether or not shapeshifting is a worthwhile justification for how their powers should work. All &quot;oh, they had this whole cool premise in shapeshifting and then disregarded it entirely&quot; until something entailing shapeshifting is brought up and it becomes &quot;oh, why should they need to shapeshift to do that&quot;.<br /> <br /> You know, on reflection, I don't know why I need to feel any kind of insecurity about the reception of Lunar Charms or general write-up. I've already said I know the reception at large was good; I've reminded myself of that by looking back at conversations where there'd be something like one person arguing vehemently against something like the witch stuff against about a dozen who were on board with it.<br /> <br /> It brought up a cogent thing Lea once said:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There will never be a universally recognized good take on Lunars, because the first take out of the gate didn't work and so everyone developed their own idiosyncratic desires for what the Lunars ought to be, and they're incompatible. <b>If you think your take is perfect and would finally settle the arguments and Show Them All if it ever reached publication, you're wrong. It wouldn't.</b> I don't know what the ratio is between people who think the Solar/Lunar bond is essential to the character type because they have OCs they're really invested in who are hung on it and would be furious if those OCs were invalidated by a Lunar take where the bond isn't a big deal vs. people who find it tiresome and would just like it to go away, but I do know that no matter which take on Lunars the game runs with, the people on the other side of that divide won't be happy.<br /> <br /> What's important is that the published take on Lunars works, somehow, so <i>some people</i> can enjoy it.<br /> <br /> &quot;The present is built on the idiosyncrasies of the past&quot; was not a quip. It's my actual answer to this argument. You can't keep something like the Lunars in undefined formless conceptual space forever; when it comes time to put something on the page, you need to make decisions, and those decisions will often be based on precedent that is more or less arbitrary.<br /> <br /> Relentless conceptual optimization is a doom spiral and <b>arguments with people who demand justification for every published creative decision are nightmares.</b> </div> </div> </div>And I see how some of the commentary here has escalated, the kind of derision against how the Charms were written and how they could be received that would be severe even from somebody who'd ever actually read them first hand. Between that and response to my attempts to emphasise what would be more conciliatory with the published Charms that there is clearly no interest in... I really should find more fulfilling things to waste my time with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-04T17:16:15-07:00","id":1543126,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1543093#post1543093\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think you may misunderstand. I'm not saying there are Charms to let you use an Intimacy, I'm saying that there are Charms with effects that key off an Intimacy being present for the shape's true identity.<br /> <br /> Notably, that kind of magic works whether the person knows you're the real deal or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I got that. That's why it felt weird. It works like the Solar Charms that hold an Intimacy that someone had over someone's head, so you can still get them to do stuff based on their sense of duty or the like.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Still, you really seem to go back and forth on whether or not shapeshifting is a worthwhile justification for how their powers should work. All &quot;oh, they had this whole cool premise in shapeshifting and then disregarded it entirely&quot; until something entailing shapeshifting is brought up and it becomes &quot;oh, why should they need to shapeshift to do that&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Well, that's actually very simple - there are a lot of different ways to go about that and I'm utterly baffled by the priorities of where the book went. Quite likely, I can't come to terms with it. Also, in the above case, it was very strongly like a Solar Charm. The long history of Lunar Charms being a Solar Charm, but limited and worse is kind of a sore point.<br /> <br /> Edit: And with regard to Latent Abilities vs. Protean Keyword special effects, you're learning two of the same thing, potentially. Then you have to keep in mind what all of your forms' special abilities are. The demand on cognitive load for Lunars seems pretty high; why make it higher?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You know, on reflection, I don't know why I need to feel any kind of insecurity about the reception of Lunar Charms or general write-up. I've already said I know the reception at large was good; I've reminded myself of that by looking back at conversations where there'd be something like one person arguing vehemently against something like the witch stuff against about a dozen who were on board with it. </div> </div> </div>And yet you decided to come back several times, now trying to get in the last word.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It brought up a cogent thing Lea once said:<br /> <br /> <br /> And I see how some of the commentary here has escalated, the kind of derision against how the Charms were written and how they could be received that would be severe even from somebody who'd ever actually read them first hand. Between that and response to my attempts to emphasise what would be more conciliatory with the published Charms that there is clearly no interest in... I really should find more fulfilling things to waste my time with. </div> </div> </div>That doesn't hit quite so hard if you have to come back to say it in the same thread. I'm not going to pursue you out of the thread to get an answer to a question that likely not even the developers or authors can provide a satisfactory answer for. The thread wasn't about that, anyway; it was about opinions on 3e Lunars, and I had a lot of those, regardless of whether or not you happen to continue posting in the thread. If you're upset that I haven't read the book in full and tend to only rely on second-hand, filtered accounts, recall why I might have had a good reason for putting off reading it. As is, I think I've managed to do a wonderful job of not actually exploding about 3e Lunars the way that I felt I should be able to and diverting the conversation away from strictly being about personal vendettas with a writer.<br /> <br /> I'd kind of like to break away, but a Fetter doesn't just resolve itself. Keeping in mind, I've been quite terribly disappointed since I got my hopes up reading about the design precepts of Alchemicals Charms all the way back in not the Autochthonians, but <b>Time of Tumult</b>. It would likely have been better not to get my hopes up again when 3e was in its pre-writing stages, or even for 2e, but those are the breaks of finding that one thing in ttrpgs that you were looking for and it being so close to matching up and all. I've been disentangling myself from it for the past six years, but very little seems to be an effective source of leverage. I keep getting pulled back. You can't just set something up with the scent of delicious food and get angry that flies show up, or shine a light in the depths and then be surprised when something chases after it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 10-05-2024, 02:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-05T13:37:59-07:00","id":1543159,"title":"","content":"Okay, I am going to respond to one other thing just because I only noticed it after seeing how it was also a focus in prior conversations:<br /> <br /> &quot;Druid&quot; never occurred to me because that is a word that is almost never used for people with magic outside the context of Dungeons and Dragons, because that game's decision to attach the name of the ancient Celtic priesthood to its collection of nature magic quasi-clerics is almost completely arbitrary.<br /> <br /> The archetype of shapeshifters has no association with druids outside of that, and any connection to Dungeons and Dragons is projected because Lunars have almost no feature in common besides the shapeshifting (which is an inexact match in the first place).<br /> <br /> And since the real world implication of druid is a religious one, if Third Edition was going to incorporate it at all, it would probably do so in similar terms to how it's putting &quot;shaman&quot; back in its proper place, rather than appropriating it for some particular kind of magic use.<br /> <br /> (Except there's not really then much need to, because druids didn't really have much to distinguish them as religious figures outside of how Romans had a particular hate on for them)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-05T19:12:43-07:00","id":1543178,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1543159#post1543159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, I am going to respond to one other thing just because I only noticed it after seeing how it was also a focus in prior conversations:<br /> <br /> &quot;Druid&quot; never occurred to me because that is a word that is almost never used for people with magic outside the context of Dungeons and Dragons, because that game's decision to attach the name of the ancient Celtic priesthood to its collection of nature magic quasi-clerics is almost completely arbitrary.<br /> <br /> The archetype of shapeshifters has no association with druids outside of that, and any connection to Dungeons and Dragons is projected because Lunars have almost no feature in common besides the shapeshifting (which is an inexact match in the first place).</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the environment that I slog through when exploring things outside of Exalted. The ampersand game is, after all, pervasive in trpgs to the point of an effective monopoly, and it shapes the expectations of people who make games even beyond that. If Exalted has become unwelcoming, everything else manages to be even worse. Apparently, everyone is horribly averse to making a suite of powers keyed toward that spot between animal shapeshifting and playable monsters.<br /> <br /> And W5? It's like someone tried to force Werewolf through a Vampire-shaped template and then deliberately poisoned the air against everything that they didn't keep so that no one new would want to touch W20 or Revised.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And since the real world implication of druid is a religious one, if Third Edition was going to incorporate it at all, it would probably do so in similar terms to how it's putting &quot;shaman&quot; back in its proper place, rather than appropriating it for some particular kind of magic use.<br /> <br /> (Except there's not really then much need to, because druids didn't really have much to distinguish them as religious figures outside of how Romans had a particular hate on for them) </div> </div> </div>I felt it was important to note because of, as noted, D&amp;D warps every other game, leaving few options for going wandering. So I end up with a lot of energy bouncing around that can't go to, say, even trying to write and/or draw fiction for Exalted on STV because I'm not sure how tolerant they would be of my ignoring vast swaths of how present and 2e material conceptualizes Lunar powers, and it just goes back in on itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 10-05-2024, 10:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-15T19:15:08-08:00","id":1545977,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1543178#post1543178\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And W5? It's like someone tried to force Werewolf through a Vampire-shaped template and then deliberately poisoned the air against everything that they didn't keep so that no one new would want to touch W20 or Revised.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm just gonna say that comparing the current rework of lunas to W5 is extremely disengious due to fact that well exalted offers much more player choice. <br /> <br /> Like did the Luna book go out and beat the fact the classic way of playing Lunas of being volient barbians is bad and dumb and you should just give up and submit to the emmculate order. No<br /> <br /> Did they reduce the splat's sigture character into resentful shell of himself. Again no. One could argue that third eds Ma-Ha-Suchi is in a much more healthy headspace than his second Ed counterpart.<br /> <br /> and at end of the day choice of how the players like to flavour they luna are up to them weather they be a witch, shape shifting barbarian or theorist warrior monk king is ultimately up to them. Which is more than I could say for w5.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","name":"MrornX","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"418"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-15T19:35:45-08:00","id":1545982,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MrornX</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1545977#post1545977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I'm just gonna say that comparing the current rework of lunas to W5 is extremely disengious due to fact that well exalted offers much more player choice.<br /> <br /> Like did the Luna book go out and beat the fact the classic way of playing Lunas of being volient barbians is bad and dumb and you should just give up and submit to the emmculate order. No<br /> <br /> Did they reduce the splat's sigture character into resentful shell of himself. Again no. One could argue that third eds Ma-Ha-Suchi is in a much more healthy headspace than his second Ed counterpart.</div> </div> </div> </div>Leviathan, in both 2e and 3e.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and at end of the day choice of how the players like to flavour they luna are up to them weather they be a witch, shape shifting barbarian or theorist warrior monk king is ultimately up to them. Which is more than I could say for w5. </div> </div> </div>I was comparing non-Lunar material to W5. The material that I called out as not being very welcoming and habitually turning away from what seemed, to me, to be an easy intersection. Shapeshifting monsters! But they have to be cursed, or sanitized into some spellcaster class, or something else that drains my enthusiasm away.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-15T19:47:09-08:00","id":1545984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1545982#post1545982\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> I was comparing non-Lunar material to W5. The material that I called out as not being very welcoming and habitually turning away from what seemed, to me, to be an easy intersection. Shapeshifting monsters! But they have to be cursed, or sanitized into some spellcaster class, or something else that drains my enthusiasm away.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well this might just be a you thing than anything else since most of the people I've know are pretty content with current state of lunars and they have the option to flavor then as shapeshifters unlike W5.<br /> <br /> Also personally usally picture the luners somewhat like the monkey king, semi devine warrior rebels which just have shapeshifting amoung they vast reprletour of power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","name":"MrornX","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"418"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-15T19:55:19-08:00","id":1545985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MrornX</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1545984#post1545984\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well this might just be a you thing than anything else since most of the people I've know are pretty content with current state of lunars and they have the option to flavor then as shapeshifters unlike W5.<br /> <br /> Also personally usally picture the luners somewhat like the monkey king, semi devine warrior rebels which just have shapeshifting amoung they vast reprletour of power.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe I covered my opinions on that earlier in the thread. No Silver Solars, please; it's a waste of a good shapeshifting hook! [/Doug Bradley]","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-18T01:04:10-08:00","id":1546063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1545982#post1545982\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Leviathan, in both 2e and 3e.</div> </div> </div> </div>Third Edition Leviathan's story of grief and a severe blow to his confidence, nursed by a thousand years of defending Luthe from the varied and mighty devils of the Undersea and developing a relationship with its inhabitants that is aloof but not totally isolated (he is at least connected enough to have fathered several children, and a regular attendant of poetry recitals in which it's possible to have audience with him) is largely a set up for his story in the time the game is played in. That being that the established timeline of the Realm's expansion into the West, preceding year zero by a century, coincides with (and is the inciting incident for) the reignition of his passion.<br /> <br /> Hence, he has a distinct position of being an ancient and venerable figure who is nevertheless playing catch up to a level of military cohesion for his dominion and mentorship to Lunar adherents that many younger shahan-yas are well established in. He's dedicated to the cause (to the point that he's kind of unthinkingly stumbled into a bloodless military dictatorship of the city, creating tension with its ruling council that he would otherwise support), but lacks experience as a teacher and leader, and still somewhat struggles to be relatable compared to so much time living in the manner of an animal. Hence, he has a unique relationship with his lieutenant Lunars such as Swims-in-Shadows, who are devoted to him for his achievements and history, but a lot better with people. It also gives him a complicated relationship with Sha'a Oka, as Leviathan would like to ally with such a prominent fellow shahan-ya of the West, but Oka finds Leviathan's prior inactivity unforgivable. <br /> <br /> Far from murderously hating Dragon-Blooded just for existing, he actually safeguards at least one prominent Water Aspect who was born in Luthe from more hostile Lunar visitors.<br /> <br /> It should be noted that Third Edition positions several of its surviving First Age Lunars as people carrying an enduring sense of grief about the Usurpation. I find it sets them apart from all other Lunars by emphasizing that for them it is not abstract history, the background behind the power structures that the Silver Pact is made to fight against. It was a loss of friends, family, the highest status and prestige, wonders and glory, their entire world. It addresses a point of not making them completely domineering figures within the Silver Pact by bringing attention to qualities (and certain distinctions in outlook created by the combination of having lived in a world that most other Lunars cannot imagine and sheer age) that render them as more distant, isolated figures, if still owed great reputation and possessed of tremendous knowledge that is worth seeking out. In that respect, Leviathan is probably set up specifically to be one of the most approachable and proactive of them.<br /> <br /> But isolated in ways that facilitate their particular plot hooks, such as how Ul of the Burning Eye keeps to himself while trying to devise a disease that will wipe out the Dragon-Blooded exclusively.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-18T16:36:33-08:00","id":1546099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1546063#post1546063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Third Edition Leviathan's story of grief and a severe blow to his confidence, nursed by a thousand years of defending Luthe from the varied and mighty devils of the Undersea and developing a relationship with its inhabitants that is aloof but not totally isolated (he is at least connected enough to have fathered several children, and a regular attendant of poetry recitals in which it's possible to have audience with him) is largely a set up for his story in the time the game is played in. That being that the established timeline of the Realm's expansion into the West, preceding year zero by a century, coincides with (and is the inciting incident for) the reignition of his passion.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find it odd that any part of Luthe or its society still endured when the call for the edition was to cut back on that kind of thing, but the whale's swam.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence, he has a distinct position of being an ancient and venerable figure who is nevertheless playing catch up to a level of military cohesion for his dominion and mentorship to Lunar adherents that many younger shahan-yas are well established in. He's dedicated to the cause (to the point that he's kind of unthinkingly stumbled into a bloodless military dictatorship of the city, creating tension with its ruling council that he would otherwise support), but lacks experience as a teacher and leader, and still somewhat struggles to be relatable compared to so much time living in the manner of an animal. </div> </div> </div>I'm going to call particular attention to this because if he were actually acting like his icon beast he'd be even more social. Orcas don't usually survive alone, act alone, or get along alone; they, along with many other toothed whales, are extremely keen on organizing themselves into peer alliance systems, family units, and family units grouped into alliance systems. Having all Lunars be loners because they're &quot;living like an animal&quot; is a lot like the continued existence of Siakal as presented.<br /> <br /> What Leviathan was in 1e was a recognized head honcho of the Solar Realm's navy (taken from him in 2e and never given back), who then sank his own fleet on his own, and perhaps also did the same to the entirety of Luthe. I feel that's metal in a way that subsequent editions can't match by saying &quot;Voila! First Age underwater city, still partially intact, with some of its old culture!&quot;, especially when you add on how he wasn't actually idle in 1e, but was solo-ing monstrous behemoths.<br /> <br /> (It's not outright stated that he personally sank Luthe, but the book said that he scuttled his own fleet, and, well, Luthe was a floating city that moved around, which is an awful lot like how modern aircraft carriers are described, so it's not much a stretch to suggest that he did it.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence, he has a unique relationship with his lieutenant Lunars such as Swims-in-Shadows, who are devoted to him for his achievements and history, but a lot better with people. It also gives him a complicated relationship with Sha'a Oka, as Leviathan would like to ally with such a prominent fellow shahan-ya of the West, but Oka finds Leviathan's prior inactivity unforgivable.<br /> <br /> Far from murderously hating Dragon-Blooded just for existing, he actually safeguards at least one prominent Water Aspect who was born in Luthe from more hostile Lunar visitors. </div> </div> </div>I don't even think that he particularly cared about DBs in 1e, but I guess we can at least both agree that 2e's portrayal was lacking on that point and that while 3e represents an improvement, I find it has way too much of the 2e story residue on it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It should be noted that Third Edition positions several of its surviving First Age Lunars as people carrying an enduring sense of grief about the Usurpation. I find it sets them apart from all other Lunars by emphasizing that for them it is not abstract history, the background behind the power structures that the Silver Pact is made to fight against. It was a loss of friends, family, the highest status and prestige, wonders and glory, their entire world. It addresses a point of not making them completely domineering figures within the Silver Pact by bringing attention to qualities (and certain distinctions in outlook created by the combination of having lived in a world that most other Lunars cannot imagine and sheer age) that render them as more distant, isolated figures, if still owed great reputation and possessed of tremendous knowledge that is worth seeking out. In that respect, Leviathan is probably set up specifically to be one of the most approachable and proactive of them.<br /> <br /> But isolated in ways that facilitate their particular plot hooks, such as how Ul of the Burning Eye keeps to himself while trying to devise a disease that will wipe out the Dragon-Blooded exclusively. </div> </div> </div>Yes, but Leviathan originally dealt with whatever trauma he had been dealing with by continuing to act as a force unto himself and continuing some manner of patrol of the oceans. All you'd need to do is make him slightly more open to hearing others out, but the chapter opening fiction told from the perspective of 1e Swims in Shadows implies exactly that, as he had decided on a plan of action and was sharing it with younger Lunars.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T00:11:31-08:00","id":1546181,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1546099#post1546099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I find it odd that any part of Luthe or its society still endured when the call for the edition was to cut back on that kind of thing, but the whale's swam.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, so a thing to understand about Sunken Luthe in Third Edition is that it is almost entirely flooded. There's almost nothing of big airtight chambers in which typical humans can survive (barring a few tiny holdouts, whose existence is almost akin to a disability). The majority of its inhabitants are aquatic beastfolk (of a large variety of animals), who live freely in the open water, that arrived in the aftermath. They possess their own distinct culture that grew up in the meantime under the circumstances.<br /> <br /> Luthe is not a surviving First Age city, it is a new community that grew in the remains of a destroyed First Age city. Much of the existing architecture has been grown into it from things like coral. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm going to call particular attention to this because if he were actually acting like his icon beast he'd be even more social. Orcas don't usually survive alone, act alone, or get along alone; they, along with many other toothed whales, are extremely keen on organizing themselves into peer alliance systems, family units, and family units grouped into alliance systems. Having all Lunars be loners because they're &quot;living like an animal&quot; is a lot like the continued existence of Siakal as presented.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well he's not Lilith, he didn't transform his mind as well as his body.<br /> <br /> If &quot;living like an animal&quot; was an unsuitable shorthand for living in the wilderness largely detached from human mores and social structures then fair, but the latter point is still the thing he was doing (that a Lunar is empowered to do in ways distinct from other Exalted) that presents an obstacle to him getting back in the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">What Leviathan was in 1e was a recognized head honcho of the Solar Realm's navy (taken from him in 2e and never given back), who then sank his own fleet on his own, and perhaps also did the same to the entirety of Luthe. I feel that's metal in a way that subsequent editions can't match by saying &quot;Voila! First Age underwater city, still partially intact, with some of its old culture!&quot;, especially when you add on how he wasn't actually idle in 1e, but was solo-ing monstrous behemoths.</div> </div> </div> </div>The behemoths are still there, it's just that now there are people on the other side of him that are part his incentivize.<br /> <br /> I can't recall offhand what the book currently says about what he did in the First Age, although I think it may have emphasized him more as hunter than admiral.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't even think that he particularly cared about DBs in 1e, but I guess we can at least both agree that 2e's portrayal was lacking on that point and that while 3e represents an improvement, I find it has way too much of the 2e story residue on it.</div> </div> </div> </div>What is the residue to which you refer?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, but Leviathan originally dealt with whatever trauma he had been dealing with by continuing to act as a force unto himself and continuing some manner of patrol of the oceans.</div> </div> </div> </div>He does have that in Third Edition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">All you'd need to do is make him slightly more open to hearing others out, but the chapter opening fiction told from the perspective of 1e Swims in Shadows implies exactly that, as he had decided on a plan of action and was sharing it with younger Lunars.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see how the description I've given of Leviathan suggests he is not hearing people out over his last century of being a more active member of the Silver Pact.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T11:01:23-08:00","id":1546201,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1546181#post1546181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Okay, so a thing to understand about Sunken Luthe in Third Edition is that it is almost entirely flooded. There's almost nothing of big airtight chambers in which typical humans can survive (barring a few tiny holdouts, whose existence is almost akin to a disability). The majority of its inhabitants are aquatic beastfolk (of a large variety of animals), who live freely in the open water, that arrived in the aftermath. They possess their own distinct culture that grew up in the meantime under the circumstances.<br /> <br /> Luthe is not a surviving First Age city, it is a new community that grew in the remains of a destroyed First Age city. Much of the existing architecture has been grown into it from things like coral.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Well he's not Lilith, he didn't transform his mind as well as his body.<br /> <br /> If &quot;living like an animal&quot; was an unsuitable shorthand for living in the wilderness largely detached from human mores and social structures then fair, but the latter point is still the thing he was doing (that a Lunar is empowered to do in ways distinct from other Exalted) that presents an obstacle to him getting back in the game.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The behemoths are still there, it's just that now there are people on the other side of him that are part his incentivize.<br /> <br /> I can't recall offhand what the book currently says about what he did in the First Age, although I think it may have emphasized him more as hunter than admiral.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> What is the residue to which you refer?</div> </div> </div> </div>The part where Leviathan is romantically or sexually active. He apparently has children, according to inkless pen, though that could very well have changed after writing, I guess.<br /> <br /> I'll be delaying any further response on this until December, when the Alchemical crowdfunding ends and they send out my Fangs at the Gate pdf (By then I'll already have MFS from the likely and imminent post-Thanksgiving Day sale on DTRPG, most likely).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T13:05:28-08:00","id":1546205,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1546201#post1546201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The part where Leviathan is romantically or sexually active. He apparently has children, according to inkless pen, though that could very well have changed after writing, I guess.</div> </div> </div> </div>Blood-Dimmed Deep remains in the book, and a very important character; she's the commander of Luthe's standing army/police force and, as a member of its ruling council, functions as her father's strongest advocate in the face of others who have reservations against the direction he's taking the city in and the undermining of their own sovereignty. She has some standing with the Silver Pact, but there's tension between her and Leviathan and his adherents, allies, and guests, as she advocates for more open warfare against the Realm, whereas he largely adheres to the general strategic policy of the Pact that calls for asymmetric fighting.<br /> <br /> That there's an issue with Leviathan having children is... news.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T13:49:06-08:00","id":1546206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1546205#post1546205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Blood-Dimmed Deep remains in the book, and a very important character; she's the commander of Luthe's standing army/police force and, as a member of its ruling council, functions as her father's strongest advocate in the face of others who have reservations against the direction he's taking the city in and the undermining of their own sovereignty. She has some standing with the Silver Pact, but there's tension between her and Leviathan and his adherents, allies, and guests, as she advocates for more open warfare against the Realm, whereas he largely adheres to the general strategic policy of the Pact that calls for asymmetric fighting.<br /> <br /> That there's an issue with Leviathan having children is... news.</div> </div> </div> </div>I suppose there's room for it, but for the most part, Leviathan was distinguished in 1e not just in not having a Solar mate, but in flat out not having an interest in sex or romance, per the passage &quot;...the sea was more a lover to him than any man or woman could be.&quot; Perhaps I'm reading it too strongly, but it does seem like Leviathan is just not someone that engages in that part of life.<br /> <br /> (He could have used neomah or another way around needing a partner, I suppose.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":".  Last minute thoughts 11-20-2024, 04:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T15:06:04-08:00","id":1546214,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1546206#post1546206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I suppose there's room for it, but for the most part, Leviathan was distinguished in 1e not just in not having a Solar mate, but in flat out not having an interest in sex or romance, per the passage &quot;...the sea was more a lover to him than any man or woman could be.&quot; Perhaps I'm reading it too strongly, but it does seem like Leviathan is just not someone that engages in that part of life.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmmm.<br /> <br /> All I can really say for that is that most Lunar characters who appeared in prior Editions have had many aspects of their personality changed, even while the key features of their premise remain consistent. I think this has generally been in service to adjusting them to the context of the revised Lunar mission statement (and in some cases dropping their more caricatured features).<br /> <br /> I won't say they're improvements, but I do think they fulfil that purpose in a way that validates the intention behind doing so.<br /> <br /> That being said, I can understand that if a person felt a strong attachment to the characterisation of any given Lunar in prior Editions, it can be a disappointment to see them rewritten like that. <br /> <br /> Likewise I can sympathise with anybody who might have read OG Leviathan as aroace representation and feel like it's a loss to shift him away from that*, particularly if there's no apparent character to fit in the slot left behind (even if there are aromantic and/or asexual characters in the Edition as a whole).<br /> <br /> Possibly. I'm not sure offhand if there are other Lunars who are, and I confess to being too lazy right now to check.<br /> <br /> As far as Solar Mates go, it's at least explicit in this Edition that not every Lunar had one (which goes beyond the fact that there are dozens more Lunars than there are Solars, as the possibility is present that not every Solar has a Mate). Although I do believe the game errs on the side of not being explicit whether or not any given character with no explicit Mate in their backstory categorically has none or has been given a blank space in which to develop that at the table.<br /> <br /> * Even if he does become transgender representation instead.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T17:21:38-08:00","id":1546222,"title":"","content":"Transmasc aroace is still aroace; as a cisdude, I don't personally feel that it's an issue, and wouldn't touch that if I were rewriting the character, but I would still put back in the lack of romantic or sexual corners. Though, I would characterize where I sit as more on the gray than as far as aro ace (dim feelings but possibly still there).<br /> <br /> My longer-lasting investment in Leviathan's characterization is probably the lack of the Solar mate. The other details took some time to key into, after listening to a lot of other people vent online and then recalling moments in my own life. And it might not even be that bad, but that Leviathan as Perfectly Straight in 2e was handled so badly, with the love triangle and &quot;Lancelot but fucks&quot; fixation on his mate's wife. It was made to be as hot a mess as possible, and boy was it. And the justification for it was &quot;he needs to have a personality and not just be an implacable force of nature&quot;, compounded with everyone saying that they preferred things that way. I can't quite recall how long it's been since my personal backlash at that particular part of 2e, but since I took pains to try and put as much as possible about 2e out of mind about 10-12 years ago, probably about that long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T22:21:10-08:00","id":1546228,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1546222#post1546222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Transmasc aroace is still aroace</div> </div> </div> </div>I... feel as though this is a comment that would imply that somebody assumed they were mutually exclusive.<br /> <br /> I did not.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T22:28:59-08:00","id":1546229,"title":"","content":"Apologies. None was intended.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-29T13:45:47-08:00","id":1548034,"title":"","content":"So, Leviathan is presented as having a solar mate in FATG and I'm calling shenanigans on the necessity of this because the consequences of the 1e take - scuttling your own fleet, as a fleet officer, with all hands aboard and the targets of your &quot;manual&quot; go at it - would be traumatizing enough to accomplish a lot of the same story beats in driving Leviathan away from whatever calm, reassuring stance of normalcy he had enjoyed beforehand. Similar to Lilith, but instead of spending time off as an owl, he's lurking around the depths as a naked, unrestrained monster of the deep.<br /> <br /> And if you really wanted to drag the bond into it, it could be a demonstration of the Terrestrial Bond from Heirs to the Shogunate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-29T19:53:21-08:00","id":1548042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1548034#post1548034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, Leviathan is presented as having a solar mate in FATG and I'm calling shenanigans on the necessity of this</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the Solar Bond occupies a tense place in the state of Exalted lore when composing a Lunars book. It's apparent that writers agree that there's not only an enduring sense of relevance to it, but to even some of what it took on in Second Edition. From that position, one might not want it to be neglected, lest it seem an irrelevant background detail (especially since a lot of its heft is still being excised from that Second Edition version). But on the other hand, yeah, you do want to not make it feel as though it's the major defining force of motivation for Lunar Exalted.<br /> <br /> (That in combination with the fact that you get very few First Age Lunars to have actually experienced the Bond, or at least at the level of intensity that might have come from a relationship spanning centuries.)<br /> <br /> For Leviathan, I think it could be as simple as somebody just thinking that such melancholy was neat.<br /> <br /> And whether or not scuttling a fleet would have been a suitable alternative source of trauma (I think it would have been fine), that seems to be a casualty of apparently removing that detail from his backstory altogether in favour of giving him a history as a hunter that is more consistent with his portrayal in the Second Age. I think there's a valid basis for that on the grounds of a bit more conservation of detail (it would seem to go along with a point of how we're otherwise told little of First Age history that has little bearing on the playable setting), that it doesn't give him a skill set that is conspicuously absent from his familiar mode of activity, and because the overall presentation of the First Age makes the idea of a Lunar as the highest ranking admiral stand out less, both on grounds of &quot;the Old Realm might not have even had a singular navy&quot; and more general Exalted distribution of authority.<br /> <br /> On top of which, well... the Lunar Charm set that has been decided upon doesn't exactly empower them in the best way to command ships in battle. Personally, I think this is a preferable thing; there's a point at which you have to decide what capabilities don't really translate well into your Attribute based Charm set, and I think an Ability as specific as &quot;Sail&quot; is an obvious choice for that. Given the state of Lunar Charms in the past, it's not as if they're losing anything that they were particularly attached to in the first place.<br /> <br /> (Really, between the Charms and the surrounding statement of &quot;Lunars can develop outside the lines defined by their Charms, but they have to rely on mundane skill to do it&quot;, I question what exactly the basis for Leviathan to have been a great admiral amongst other Exalted exactly was in First Edition)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And if you really wanted to drag the bond into it, it could be a demonstration of the Terrestrial Bond from Heirs to the Shogunate.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perhaps. The publication dates are a bit apart, but I see they have enough authors in common that people working on Lunars may well have been aware of such a thing being part of the other book. Possibly it would have been a tricky place to introduce and then explain it.<br /> <br /> Particularly given the writing in Heirs that seems to want to frame it as an exceptional plot hook for the future, rather than a reasonably established part of Lunar and Terrestrial lore. That kind of makes the only reason to back trace it into Leviathan to be looking for an alternative to the Solar Bond just to <i>be</i> an alternative to the Solar Bond, but I don't think they were proceeding from the assumption that their depiction of Leviathan would draw antipathy.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, sure, he doesn't need to be mystically bonded to somebody for their loss to have that impact on him. But I suppose that kind of rolls back around to &quot;there will be plenty of Lunars who never even met a Solar that can have such backstories&quot;.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-29T22:19:50-08:00","id":1548043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1548042#post1548042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think the Solar Bond occupies a tense place in the state of Exalted lore when composing a Lunars book. It's apparent that writers agree that there's not only an enduring sense of relevance to it, but to even some of what it took on in Second Edition. From that position, one might not want it to be neglected, lest it seem an irrelevant background detail (especially since a lot of its heft is still being excised from that Second Edition version). But on the other hand, yeah, you do want to not make it feel as though it's the major defining force of motivation for Lunar Exalted.<br /> <br /> (That in combination with the fact that you get very few First Age Lunars to have actually experienced the Bond, or at least at the level of intensity that might have come from a relationship spanning centuries.)<br /> <br /> For Leviathan, I think it could be as simple as somebody just thinking that such melancholy was neat.<br /> <br /> And whether or not scuttling a fleet would have been a suitable alternative source of trauma (I think it would have been fine), that seems to be a casualty of apparently removing that detail from his backstory altogether in favour of giving him a history as a hunter that is more consistent with his portrayal in the Second Age. I think there's a valid basis for that on the grounds of a bit more conservation of detail (it would seem to go along with a point of how we're otherwise told little of First Age history that has little bearing on the playable setting), that it doesn't give him a skill set that is conspicuously absent from his familiar mode of activity, and because the overall presentation of the First Age makes the idea of a Lunar as the highest ranking admiral stand out less, both on grounds of &quot;the Old Realm might not have even had a singular navy&quot; and more general Exalted distribution of authority.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even if not the highest ranking admiral because they wanted to emphasize Solar favoritism, he was still a fleet officer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On top of which, well... the Lunar Charm set that has been decided upon doesn't exactly empower them in the best way to command ships in battle. Personally, I think this is a preferable thing; there's a point at which you have to decide what capabilities don't really translate well into your Attribute based Charm set, and I think an Ability as specific as &quot;Sail&quot; is an obvious choice for that. Given the state of Lunar Charms in the past, it's not as if they're losing anything that they were particularly attached to in the first place. </div> </div> </div>All the potential of Selenographic Transmission Technique lost, like tears in the rain...<br /> <br /> I think that anything that could command troops could also facilitate commanding troops. Particularly if the Lunar is using whale calls, since sound goes even faster in the water.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (Really, between the Charms and the surrounding statement of &quot;Lunars can develop outside the lines defined by their Charms, but they have to rely on mundane skill to do it&quot;, I question what exactly the basis for Leviathan to have been a great admiral amongst other Exalted exactly was in First Edition) </div> </div> </div>I did already mention that I think that this sells a lot of other potential setups way too short. Again, Alchemicals get a pass for everything, even vacuum tubes that ward against malign spirits, but Lunars can't get anything even remotely akin to zoacrystals from Guyver.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Perhaps. The publication dates are a bit apart, but I see they have enough authors in common that people working on Lunars may well have been aware of such a thing being part of the other book. Possibly it would have been a tricky place to introduce and then explain it.<br /> <br /> Particularly given the writing in Heirs that seems to want to frame it as an exceptional plot hook for the future, rather than a reasonably established part of Lunar and Terrestrial lore. That kind of makes the only reason to back trace it into Leviathan to be looking for an alternative to the Solar Bond just to <i>be</i> an alternative to the Solar Bond, but I don't think they were proceeding from the assumption that their depiction of Leviathan would draw antipathy.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, sure, he doesn't need to be mystically bonded to somebody for their loss to have that impact on him. But I suppose that kind of rolls back around to &quot;there will be plenty of Lunars who never even met a Solar that can have such backstories&quot;. </div> </div> </div>I'm still pondering the purpose of setting things back to having more Lunars than Solars if you're not also going to go back to some Lunars not having Solar mates.<br /> <br /> Also, this is kind of tangential, but many, many hours of Unicorn Overlord have me questioning if it has to be an intrinsic part of two Exalted. Perhaps you could just have Artifacts carried by folks with mutual Ties that get power from said Ties and magical pilgrimages.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-09T23:09:49-08:00","id":1548355,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1548043#post1548043\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Even if not the highest ranking admiral because they wanted to emphasize Solar favoritism, he was still a fleet officer.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm here talking about how there wasn't some worldwide navy for anybody to be highest ranking admiral of, and the prospect for Dragon-Blooded to figure highly there, and you talk about Solar favoritism.<br /> <br /> And are you making that fleet officer comment referring to this Edition? Because I do not see any reference in the write ups to that.<br /> <br /> My point has been that, having arrived at a particular idea for what Lunar powers should generally be, it's understandable and reasonable for characters to have been written with consistency to it, and that the value of Leviathan as a significant naval officer is not high enough to draw the writing of Charms around it, especially for something as Ability specific as sailing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that anything that could command troops could also facilitate commanding troops.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, but there's a specific naval combat system that Solars and Dragon-Blooded have Charms to engage with, and Lunars do not.<br /> <br /> That said, I can see how many Lunar Charms, including things that arising from their particular idiom for how they might project their voice, might be applied to the position. There might not be a specifically admiral take on being a Lunar, but I can see leverage of certain powers to create a distinctly Lunar take on being an admiral.<br /> <br /> Did Leviathan need to be that admiral? I think not necessarily, and especially if he's not supposed to have done it for more than a thousand years anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, Alchemicals get a pass for everything, even vacuum tubes that ward against malign spirits, but Lunars can't get anything even remotely akin to zoacrystals from Guyver.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is the complaint here that Alchemicals get a broader remit than Lunars do, or just back in the idea of how far they can apply the premise of powers derived from physically added on modules?<br /> <br /> Either way, I think it doesn't really pay to compare the two. Alchemicals have their own thing going on, and I don't think it pays to approach Lunars from a direction of &quot;they should also be able to do just about anything, just with the right body modification to justify it&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm still pondering the purpose of setting things back to having more Lunars than Solars if you're not also going to go back to some Lunars not having Solar mates.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ma-Ha-Suchi and Raksi don't make reference to having had Solar Mates, while having lived in the First Age.<br /> <br /> There's more Lunars than Solars, but probably still most Lunars have Solar Mates, so I think it is not unreasonable that of that scant handful of written Lunars who have ever so much as met any Solar, let alone enough to find their mate amongst them, most but not all would have had mates.<br /> <br /> And as I said before, if you don't use that scant handful of Lunars who have ever even met a Solar to provide some fiction to elaborate on what the bond is like, who else are you going to do it with?<br /> <br /> Most other Lunar characters don't lock it in one way or another, after all, so one is free to either have Sha'a Oka be one of those with no bond, or to get to say &quot;you have a mate, and it is me&quot;.<br /> <br /> That latter also pointing to how the most significant reason is probably not to be found in other characters but for the player who categorically wants to play a Lunar without that as a plot hook. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-10T01:29:27-08:00","id":1548356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1548355#post1548355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm here talking about how there wasn't some worldwide navy for anybody to be highest ranking admiral of, and the prospect for Dragon-Blooded to figure highly there, and you talk about Solar favoritism.</div> </div> </div> </div>We are talking about bits and pieces of different editions at the same time here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And are you making that fleet officer comment referring to this Edition? Because I do not see any reference in the write ups to that. </div> </div> </div>Serious downgrade #2, there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My point has been that, having arrived at a particular idea for what Lunar powers should generally be, it's understandable and reasonable for characters to have been written with consistency to it, and that the value of Leviathan as a significant naval officer is not high enough to draw the writing of Charms around it, especially for something as Ability specific as sailing. </div> </div> </div>In fairness, it's also extremely Attribute-specific, as all of the naval stratagem rolls use Wits.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Okay, but there's a specific naval combat system that Solars and Dragon-Blooded have Charms to engage with, and Lunars do not.<br /> <br /> That said, I can see how many Lunar Charms, including things that arising from their particular idiom for how they might project their voice, might be applied to the position. There might not be a specifically admiral take on being a Lunar, but I can see leverage of certain powers to create a distinctly Lunar take on being an admiral.<br /> <br /> Did Leviathan need to be that admiral? I think not necessarily, and especially if he's not supposed to have done it for more than a thousand years anyway. </div> </div> </div>I guess none of the Lunars needed to have any kind of status in the First Age, then. Even though that's rather counter to about half the purpose of listing The White Rose in the inspirational works.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is the complaint here that Alchemicals get a broader remit than Lunars do, or just back in the idea of how far they can apply the premise of powers derived from physically added on modules? </div> </div> </div>Both.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Either way, I think it doesn't really pay to compare the two. Alchemicals have their own thing going on, and I don't think it pays to approach Lunars from a direction of &quot;they should also be able to do just about anything, just with the right body modification to justify it&quot;. </div> </div> </div>A thing that's a more direct foil to the Lunars' thing than the Solars will ever be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ma-Ha-Suchi and Raksi don't make reference to having had Solar Mates, while having lived in the First Age. </div> </div> </div>MHS actually had one back in 1e. Not sure why that didn't carry over.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's more Lunars than Solars, but probably still most Lunars have Solar Mates, so I think it is not unreasonable that of that scant handful of written Lunars who have ever so much as met any Solar, let alone enough to find their mate amongst them, most but not all would have had mates. </div> </div> </div>I don't think that we can assume that, but Leviathan's handling in 2e was such a horrid plot tumor that I'm on the extremely negative side of this. If not mentioned, assume no Solar.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And as I said before, if you don't use that scant handful of Lunars who have ever even met a Solar to provide some fiction to elaborate on what the bond is like, who else are you going to do it with? </div> </div> </div>Someone other than Leviathan. Anyone but Leviathan.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most other Lunar characters don't lock it in one way or another, after all, so one is free to either have Sha'a Oka be one of those with no bond, or to get to say &quot;you have a mate, and it is me&quot;.<br /> <br /> That latter also pointing to how the most significant reason is probably not to be found in other characters but for the player who categorically wants to play a Lunar without that as a plot hook.<br /> </div> </div> </div>Not locking it in reminds me of Stephen Lea Sheppard's discussion of spices really needing to be included in a recipe to get the proper flavor out of them. So I suppose it's not nearly so important to most of the Lunars to be paired with Solars, after all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T11:25:52-08:00","id":1548819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1548356#post1548356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> We are talking about bits and pieces of different editions at the same time here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even if you dislike Third Edition Leviathan, it would help to have a discussion about that if it remained on point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">In fairness, it's also extremely Attribute-specific, as all of the naval stratagem rolls use Wits.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think in terms of its imagery, process, and consequences, the Ability predominates a lot more in that equation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess none of the Lunars needed to have any kind of status in the First Age, then.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's such an excluded middle kind of statement, what's the point of it?<br /> <br /> I keep trying to have this discussion in good faith, and you repeatedly fall into bitter rejoinders and attempts at point scoring.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Both.</div> </div> </div> </div>I put the broad remit of Alchemicals largely into a matter of how their primary activity is in a self-contained setting where they have both greater need and more storytelling benefits out of covering a broader base. And I put the idea that they're given a means of doing most things through the bodily modification on the point that the novelty of cybernetic implants as Charms is carrying a lot of the weight in the first place.<br /> <br /> That and it lets the focus rest more on them as Champions of the Nations then trying to figure out specific robot themed things to do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A thing that's a more direct foil to the Lunars' thing than the Solars will ever be.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even if Lunars were designed more to your tastes, would there be anything thematic or characteristic to them, anything that felt like it would create a particular kind of personality or mythos for how their powers were expressed and what they were directed towards? Or would it be just purely functional stuff with a particular aesthetic to it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">MHS actually had one back in 1e. Not sure why that didn't carry over.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't get this at all. You complain about the idea of all the First Age Lunars having Solar Mates, and then when one is written seemingly without it you find that inexplicable?<br /> <br /> Do you not think they might have not carried it over specifically for the sake of doing the thing that you were otherwise complaining about them not doing?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think that we can assume that</div> </div> </div> </div>Which thing do you think can be not assumed, that there's intent to portray most Solars and Lunars having bonds, or that it's reasonable to assume numbers that make it statistically likely that surviving First Age elders would have had mates?<br /> <br /> If the former, it's still portrayed as something that exerted a significant reshaping of the Exalted political landscape, so I think there's foundation for it being significant. If the latter, well... that most First Age Lunars have history with Solars is a fact of how it was written, so one proceeds from there.<br /> <br /> However, on reflection, I see the fallacy in making it a matter of statistics. The narrative value in having a variety of examples of what the bonded relationship was like is a higher priority, so even if there were only ever twenty Solars and Lunars who had ever been bonded, one ought to skew in favour of those having survivors so that there are some examples rather than no examples. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If not mentioned, assume no Solar.</div> </div> </div> </div>Given that's going to bias in favour of the fact that most Lunars have never met a Solar, even if they're over a thousand years old, why not just personally houserule or headcanon the bond away if it's so undesirable?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Someone other than Leviathan. Anyone but Leviathan.</div> </div> </div> </div>Setting aside the question of backstory elements such as Solar mates and whether or not he was an admirable, are the portrayals of Leviathan in the modern day of First and Third Edition really so incompatible that a person who was remarkably attached to the former would not want to emphasise what has merit in the portrayal of the latter? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not locking it in reminds me of Stephen Lea Sheppard's discussion of spices really needing to be included in a recipe to get the proper flavor out of them. So I suppose it's not nearly so important to most of the Lunars to be paired with Solars, after all.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's important enough to be a part of the overall Lunar and First Age backstory, the write ups of several characters old enough to have remembered it, and the mechanics of numerous Charms (which allow it to function through what would often otherwise be Defining Intimacies).<br /> <br /> But sure, its importance probably does fall slightly short of empowering the players to decide whether or not any given Lunar who is younger than the Usurpation is bonded to a Solar. As fit into an overall milieu of making several important things about the setting open-ended for the benefit of the table.<br /> <br /> Although even those considerations probably take a bit of a back seat to the foundational setting concept that the number of living Solars throughout the lifetime of all but the eldest Lunars have been outnumbered by Lunars by about twenty to one. Unless one wanted to propose that the write-up of Sha'a Oka should include a perfunctory aside on whether or not his Essence carries a potential bond to somebody that has been locked in a box out among the stars for a millennium.<br /> <br /> For the benefit of somebody that would then complain about all of the Lunars getting Solar Bonds, apparently.<br /> <br /> This, incidentally, is the kind of thing I was referring to above that has been making these kinds of conversations so frustrating. I bring up something about how the Lunars are actually written to try and find some cordial middle ground, some application of the published material that can be satisfying to multiple perspectives, and you turn it into something backhanded and undermining.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-01T22:40:30-08:00","id":1548864,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1542327-opinions-on-3e-lunars?p=1548819#post1548819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Even if you dislike Third Edition Leviathan, it would help to have a discussion about that if it remained on point.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am still shaking off rust and repairing directories in my head.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think in terms of its imagery, process, and consequences, the Ability predominates a lot more in that equation. </div> </div> </div>Unquestionably, but there's far from a complete void of conceptual space for Lunars, who command swarms of oceanic creatures that can encrust themselves on ships. Claim a ship as territory; claim a large marine animal as territory and use Ride to direct them for this purpose; make your sea life-encrusted ships look like floating derelicts until it's too late for incautious observers; send swarms of wood-boring bivavles into the water to degrade anyone who gets near your territory (including ships).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's such an excluded middle kind of statement, what's the point of it?<br /> <br /> I keep trying to have this discussion in good faith, and you repeatedly fall into bitter rejoinders and attempts at point scoring. </div> </div> </div>I've had over twenty years to steep into a concentrated solution of tannins.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I put the broad remit of Alchemicals largely into a matter of how their primary activity is in a self-contained setting where they have both greater need and more storytelling benefits out of covering a broader base. And I put the idea that they're given a means of doing most things through the bodily modification on the point that the novelty of cybernetic implants as Charms is carrying a lot of the weight in the first place.<br /> <br /> That and it lets the focus rest more on them as Champions of the Nations then trying to figure out specific robot themed things to do. </div> </div> </div>(This was edited)<br /> <br /> Alchemicals have been growing steadily less self-contained and stand-alone as the line has gone forward. With EX3, they are at the least stand-alone that they can be, since you can find a dormant ancient Alchemical in ruins that are older than old. As such, this isn't really a very strong argument for giving them a wizard's allowance for making shit up while Lunars get stuck with the least favorable interpretation of altering their bodies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even if Lunars were designed more to your tastes, would there be anything thematic or characteristic to them, anything that felt like it would create a particular kind of personality or mythos for how their powers were expressed and what they were directed towards? Or would it be just purely functional stuff with a particular aesthetic to it? </div> </div> </div>This is aparticularly infuriating question to ask because I basically laid down my thoughts on it already, and don't see any value in extensive exploration of mythoi via Charms. That resulted in Infernal Charms being up their own ass, recursively and repeatedly, and I would prefer it if the system details facilitated the meta role of the Lunars, that being that they're animal shapeshifters in their niche. That's what they do, and not taking advantage of it, as I asserted some time ago, is a terrible waste of a theme.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't get this at all. You complain about the idea of all the First Age Lunars having Solar Mates, and then when one is written seemingly without it you find that inexplicable? </div> </div> </div>The decision about who and what gets changed between editions is baffling to me, yes. What was set down in 2012 changed a lot and I don't have my finger on it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Do you not think they might have not carried it over specifically for the sake of doing the thing that you were otherwise complaining about them not doing? </div> </div> </div>Leviathan's characterization has too many reminders of 2e Lunar characterization for me, and 2e Lunar characterization was... a series of choices that mostly didn't get repeated, which is good, because MHS doesn't need to be an ontological evil that obviously needs to die. If Raksi didn't stay a truly terrible example of how to handle everything about her, why keep Leviathan as moping for centuries?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which thing do you think can be not assumed, that there's intent to portray most Solars and Lunars having bonds, or that it's reasonable to assume numbers that make it statistically likely that surviving First Age elders would have had mates?<br /> <br /> If the former, it's still portrayed as something that exerted a significant reshaping of the Exalted political landscape, so I think there's foundation for it being significant. If the latter, well... that most First Age Lunars have history with Solars is a fact of how it was written, so one proceeds from there.<br /> <br /> However, on reflection, I see the fallacy in making it a matter of statistics. The narrative value in having a variety of examples of what the bonded relationship was like is a higher priority, so even if there were only ever twenty Solars and Lunars who had ever been bonded, one ought to skew in favour of those having survivors so that there are some examples rather than no examples. </div> </div> </div>All the enthusiasm for it drained out of me about the millionth time that someone cited something about Gilgamesh and Enkidu. I have no fucks to give about it, so material that uses it is something I skip over. That can add up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Given that's going to bias in favour of the fact that most Lunars have never met a Solar, even if they're over a thousand years old, why not just personally houserule or headcanon the bond away if it's so undesirable? </div> </div> </div>You can houserule anything. Rule 0 Fallacy ahoy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Setting aside the question of backstory elements such as Solar mates and whether or not he was an admirable, are the portrayals of Leviathan in the modern day of First and Third Edition really so incompatible that a person who was remarkably attached to the former would not want to emphasise what has merit in the portrayal of the latter? </div> </div> </div>For one thing, Luthe was completely wrecked in 1e and devoid of inhabitants; it marked where Leviathan contemplated and planned, and was basically his exclusive territory that would probably only ever see other Lunars invited to attend (everyone else tends to drown way too easily). There was also a sense that he was a really scary person even to other Lunars, and that there wasn't really very much in the way of sadness driving him to hunt, but rather that it was more a form of reverence for Luna (as the sacred hunt was in 1e, as opposed to the default ritual for gaining shapes).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's important enough to be a part of the overall Lunar and First Age backstory, the write ups of several characters old enough to have remembered it, and the mechanics of numerous Charms (which allow it to function through what would often otherwise be Defining Intimacies). </div> </div> </div>I also have related misgivings with that stemming from:<br /> <br /> - The bond to the Solars already being so important on a large scale level that it caused Lunars to change when the Solars died. With that much influence, how do we parse the importance of individual bonds?<br /> - If the Lunars repeatedly altered their Exaltations and the way they worked, the repetition involved with this from Divine Uprising to First Age and First Age to Second kind of loses a bit of its grandeur. Change once? Momentous. Changed twice more? That's making the feat look a bit less impressive.<br /> - The word count sacrificed to make the bond sit front and center, which makes it harder to ignore and houserule out.<br /> <br /> I can actually keep citing off-putting portions all day, with one more example not directly related to this being the meme about how Lunars in 2e couldn't accomplish anything without dying. There is a Charm that sacrifices heart's blood forms (a thing not seen since an Alchemicals 1e adventure that was not well-liked for requiring steep prices out of characters as the price of admittance) to play dead as that form. Gives way too much independent substance to forms that the Lunar can turn into, much like the one that lets you call a familiar up with a similar cost.<br /> <br /> And they kept the moonsilver claws out for some reason, even though Invulnerable Moonsilver Carapace explicitly works with moonsilver. Claws of light is a concept I gave up on as not particularly exciting ages ago.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But sure, its importance probably does fall slightly short of empowering the players to decide whether or not any given Lunar who is younger than the Usurpation is bonded to a Solar. As fit into an overall milieu of making several important things about the setting open-ended for the benefit of the table.<br /> <br /> Although even those considerations probably take a bit of a back seat to the foundational setting concept that the number of living Solars throughout the lifetime of all but the eldest Lunars have been outnumbered by Lunars by about twenty to one. Unless one wanted to propose that the write-up of Sha'a Oka should include a perfunctory aside on whether or not his Essence carries a potential bond to somebody that has been locked in a box out among the stars for a millennium.<br /> <br /> For the benefit of somebody that would then complain about all of the Lunars getting Solar Bonds, apparently. </div> </div> </div>Have you been getting into my concentrated tannin solutions and heinous amounts of salt? Those are for me!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This, incidentally, is the kind of thing I was referring to above that has been making these kinds of conversations so frustrating. I bring up something about how the Lunars are actually written to try and find some cordial middle ground, some application of the published material that can be satisfying to multiple perspectives, and you turn it into something backhanded and undermining. </div> </div> </div>I told you at the start that we have a fundamental breakdown that makes actual middle ground impossible to find. You're quite pleased with Lunars as is; I would extensively change them based on a vastly different set of priorities and preferred operations. At some point during the burrowing in these conversations, you're going to wind up repeating points that my viewpoint treats as given and getting maddened by them just as I end up shaking my head at, for one example, the notion that Lunar Charms need to be open-ended to the point where most lack in-depth descriptions - though, to be fair, a number of them do have somewhat stronger establishing text! It was a pleasant surprise!<br /> <br /> They also contain shadow soul stuff and a sideline about making your reflection into a familiar spirit. Again, not something I'd ever get even on characters that were hypothetically given to those Attributes. There's zero appeal in it for me. All of the appeal is in the animal shapeshifting. I've been waiting for some system or another to grant things like Shadowrun Paranormal Animal powers and related concepts to characters for literal decades at this point, and damned if it's something that most game designers seem oddly tight-fisted about. (And which, in a number of systems, comes with the assumption that you are a spellcaster, working this through spells, as opposed to it just being what you do).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 03-02-2025, 06:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-19T16:22:41-07:00","id":1549451,"title":"","content":"In retrospect, and with the benefit of a few clips I've seen crop up in the Facebook feed I'm starting to pay attention to again, I also suspect that a big part of why writers find a basis to write Alchemicals as they have and readers turn out to be receptive to it is because the general vibe of a modular body that can be endowed with implants that unfurl and do basically anything has a strong literary pedigree in the form of cyborg anime.<br /> <br /> One may argue that the pedigree of potential inspirations for the Lunar Exalted is broader than what has been used for them on this occasion (or ever), but probably still not nearly so broad as that.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I think that makes it actually pretty impressive that they manage to have the sense of range and versatility that they do while still feeling tightly focused conceptually. To me, that's part of what contributes to the sense of how they'd be perceived in-character; that a Lunar has a vibe of being a rougher, more specific and less fanciful kind of character than a Solar or Sidereal or even Dragon-Blood, and yet exerts themselves within those bounds in a manner that feels like a credible peer of the other Celestials and terror for the Terrestrials.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-20T21:42:04-07:00","id":1549470,"title":"","content":"If Last Warrior's Unity is any indication, Lunar inspirations can be plenty broad along very similar lines. The Charm is literally the D&amp;D psionics power <i>graft weapon</i>, and Werewolf: the Last Warrior was a last minute reskin of an X-Men game after Data East lost the rights to do X-Men games. As such, the gates are open to pillage concepts from psychometabolism.<br /> <br /> (If you're wondering at why I would chafe at a caster association but not with association with psionics users, the latter's focus had a history of being very different from the former. And, quite frankly, a lot of people still find it plenty different, since they're willing to accept wizards, priests, and sorcerers but not psionics.)<br /> <br /> Getting a POD of the Lunar book has made for smoother going. Having gone through the Appearance Charms, they're mostly things that were badly needed back in 1e, but the text of Subtle Chameleon Practice struck me as intensely weird for comparing camouflage to a reflection. I can't accept that metaphor as anything but horribly tortured, and the path of that Charm into moving around as a reflection and making a god out of your reflection about the same. It seems like a lot of concentration on a concept tangential to their being moon people, which would be much better explored in an Exigent or even one of the God-Blooded.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}